jan Kumar Dey, SDPO Deepak Kumar Mishra, and IIC Sitanshu Sekhar Mandhata -- confirmed the recovery of the murder weapon, mobile phone, and motorcycle. The accused has been forwarded to court.ç||!5Å1CÇ- ó	95151458492026-10-22T14:46:26ZMob mistakes fruit vendor for phone thief in Bengaluru, assaults himThe Times of Indiatimesofindia.indiatimes.comhttps://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/bengaluru/mob-mistakes-fruit-vendor-for-phone-thief-in-bengaluru-assaults-him/articleshow/135121414.cmsengIndiaø‚rrrrrrBengaluru: A mobile phone falling to the ground after a minor mishap cost a Chickpet fruit vendor dear. A group of 8-10 people allegedly mistook him and his friend for thieves and attacked them. The vendor, Shahid Khan, 19, suffered injuries to his head and face in the assault.The teenager said he was riding his two-wheeler from Majestic towards RT Street in Chickpet with friend Fardeen Pasha alias Kaalu around 7pm on Oct 19, when the vehicle accidentally brushed against an unidentified man.The man's mobile phone fell to the ground. When Pasha picked it up to return it, passersby mistook the duo for phone thieves. A group allegedly began abusing them before turning on Khan and assaulting him. While some punched him near the eyes, others hit him on the head with their phones, leaving him bleeding, the complaint said.Khan sought action against the unidentified group for allegedly abusing and assaulting him. City Market police registered a case under BNS Sections 115 (voluntarily causing hurt), 118 (voluntarily causing hurt or grievous hurt by dangerous weapons or means), 119 (every member of an unlawful assembly guilty of an offence committed in prosecution of its common object) and 352 (intentional insult with intent to provoke a breach of peace).A senior officer said, "We have verified the CCTV footage from the surrounding buildings and are making efforts to trace the assaulters. The injured man is out of danger and has no criminal background."    -area lender posted strong net interest margin, improved credit quality and continued capital returns. The stock was little changed, last trading at $24.83, down 0.18% from the previous close of $24.87 and near the upper end of its 52-week range.Key Takeaways* EPS of $0.56 missed the $0.58 consensus estimate by $0.02, or 3.45%.* Revenue of $97.1 million fell short of the $98.22 million forecast by $1.12 million, or 1.14%.* Net interest margin rose to 5.27% on a tax-equivalent basis, up 4 basis points from the prior quarter.* Credit quality improved sharply, with criticized loans down 38% and classified loans at their lowest level since before the pandemic.* Management said a new Ducati USA partnership should lift powersport lending in the fourth quarter and more meaningfully in 2027.* The company raised its common dividend by 14% and continued share repurchases.Company PerformanceOld Second said it delivered a solid quarter despite elevated charge-offs tied to a single legacy credit relationship. The bank earned $28.9 million, or $0.56 a diluted share, on a GAAP basis. Adjusted net income, which excludes mortgage servicing rights valuation adjustments, was $28.5 million, or $0.55 a share.Profitability remained strong. Return on assets was 1.69%, and return on average tangible common equity reached 15.77%. The efficiency ratio was 52.34%, or 52.06% on an adjusted tax-equivalent basis.The quarter also showed continued strength in core banking income. Net interest income rose to $84 million from $83.3 million in the second quarter and was up $1.3 million from a year earlier. Management said the company benefited from higher loan yields, lower deposit costs than a year ago and a strong loan repricing profile.Financial Highlights* Net income: $28.9 million, or $0.56 a diluted share, versus $0.55 adjusted EPS.* Adjusted net income: $28.5 million, or $0.55 a share.* Revenue: $97.1 million, below the $98.22 million estimate.* Net interest income: $84 million, up $700,000 sequentially and $1.3 million year over year.* Net interest margin: 5.27%, up from 5.23% in the second quarter and 5.05% a year earlier.* Return on assets: 1.69%.* Return on average tangible common equity: 15.77%.* Efficiency ratio: 52.34%, versus 52.06% on an adjusted tax-equivalent basis.* Tangible book value per share: $14.75, down slightly from $14.77 in the prior quarter.* Loan-to-deposit ratio: 98.3%, up from 96.4% at the end of June.Earnings vs. ForecastOld Second missed analyst expectations on both profit and revenue, but the gap was modest. EPS came in $0.02 below the consensus estimate of $0.58, while revenue missed by about $1.12 million.The earnings miss of 3.45% was small in absolute terms. For a bank, investors often look beyond the headline miss and focus on margin, credit and capital trends. On those measures, the quarter was generally constructive. Net interest margin expanded, credit metrics improved and the company continued to return capital to shareholders.The revenue shortfall of 1.14% was also limited. The results suggest the market may be more concerned with the slight pressure from deposit competition and the near-term guidance for some margin giveback than with the size of the miss itself.Market ReactionThe stock showed little immediate movement after the report. Shares were last at $24.83, down 0.18% from the previous close of $24.87. That leaves the stock about 4.7% below its 52-week high of $26.06 and roughly 45.1% above its 52-week low of $17.11.The muted reaction suggests investors were balancing the small earnings miss against the bank's stronger underlying trends. The stock's position near the top of its yearly range also indicates that expectations were already elevated heading into the report. No unusual trading volum                                                                                                                                                                               