 in a series of suspected attacks targeting government establishments in the city, officials said.The incident occurred in the early hours of the day in the Langkyrding area of East Khasi Hills district. No structural damage to the building was reported, Superintendent of Police Vivek Syiem said.According to the police officer, the security guard stationed inside the building was unaware of the incident until Thursday morning. Police are examining CCTV footage to identify the suspects, while a case is being registered in connection with the attack. Patrolling has also been intensified in the area, Syiem said.The incident came a day after a similar attack was reported at the Health Department office in the Laitumkhrah area of Shillong, raising fresh concerns over the security of government establishments. The latest incidents follow a series of suspected petrol bomb attacks and arson cases reported across Shillong in recent months.On October 20, three suspected petrol bomb incidents were reported at Kench's Trace, Keating Road and Laitumkhrah, according to police. The attacks prompted authorities to step up security arrangements and intensify surveillance across the city.In September, a series of attacks targeted government offices, police facilities and vehicles. The Directorate of Mineral Resources, Lumdiengjri police station, the Directorate of Health Services and a police booth at Nongmynsong were among the locations affected by incidents involving petrol bombs and arson.The September attacks prompted the state government and police authorities to review security arrangements, increase patrolling and examine CCTV footage to identify those responsible.Earlier, in August, suspected miscreants also hurled petrol bombs at a National People's Party office in Shillong and a police outpost at Nongmynsong amid heightened tensions following violence during a Khasi Students' Union rally.Police have not publicly established whether the latest incidents are connected or identified a common motive behind the attacks.Investigations are continuing, with authorities seeking to identify the perpetrators and prevent further incidents.   & ∂&                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        °KÅ!5Å;''Ç  æM95151088812026-10-22T14:18:20ZEarnings call transcript: Visteon Q3 2026 profit miss weighs on shares By Investing.comInvesting.cominvesting.comhttps://www.investing.com/news/transcripts/earnings-call-transcript-visteon-q3-2026-profit-miss-weighs-on-shares-93CH-4966635engIndia?¢ Visteon reported higher third-quarter sales and said its core business remained resilient, but a large warranty charge pushed earnings far below Wall Street expectations and sent the stock l   lãÅ!5Å1CÇ ëg95151244592026-10-22T14:30:01ZOne more dead in out-of-control bus collision in CoimbatoreThe Times of Indiatimesofindia.indiatimes.comhttps://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/coimbatore/one-more-dead-in-out-of-control-bus-collision-in-coimbatore/articleshow/135121221.cmsengIndiaøÂuuuuuvCOIMBATORE: One more man died in a Tamil Nadu State Transport Corporation (TNSTC) bus collision that happened after the driver died of a suspected heart attack while driving near Kuniyamuthur on Wednesday.While an autorickshaw driver died after the out-of-control bus hit his vehicle, S Abbas, 46, of Sunnambu Kalavai on the Palakkad Main Road, succumbed to his injuries on Thursday while undergoing treatment in Coimbatore Medical College Hospital.Police said the TNSTC bus, carrying more than 40 passengers, was heading from Palathurai to Ukkadam on Wednesday morning. When the bus reached the Sunnambu Kalavai area near Kuniyamuthur, driver T Martin, 54, of Jothi Nagar in Singanallur complained of an acute chest pain and collapsed in his seat within moments.The out-of-control bus veered erratically across the busy road, crashing into a car, an autorickshaw, and a few two-wheelers ahead. The autorickshaw was completely crushed, killing its driver, Noor Mohammad, 51, of Annamma Naicker Street in Kuniyamuthur, on the spot. Motorcycle rider Abbas, who suffered grievous injuries in the collision, died in CMCH on Thursday.éFÅ !5Å1#-Åk òg95151416362026-10-22T14:43:15ZCI Amritsar busts cross-border drug racket, 4 held with 10 kg heroin - The TribuneThe Tribunetribuneindia.comhttps://www.tribuneindia.com/news/amritsar/ci-amritsar-busts-cross-border-drug-racket-4-held-with-10-kg-heroin/engIndiaø”SSSSSTThe Counter Intelligence (CI), Amritsar, has arrested four alleged drug smugglers and seized 10 kg heroin from their possession.With their arrest, CI claimed to have busted a suspected narcotics smuggling network allegedly operated in coordination with foreign-based handlers.Those arrested have been identified as Sukhraj Singh of Hargobind Avenue, Ajitpal Singh alias Mota of Khandwala, Anmolpreet Singh of Jawahar Nagar and Robin Gill of Chogawan village.The police also impounded a Hero Splendor motorcycle and a black Honda Activa scooter allegedly used to transport the contraband.DGP Gaurav Yadav said preliminary investigations indicated that the accused were allegedly working on the directions of foreign-based smugglers and had been receiving heroin consignments for further distribution in Punjab.According to the police, the CI team received a tip-off that the four suspects had retrieved a large consignment of heroin on the instructions of a foreign-based handler and were planning to deliver it to another party.Acting on the information, the police intercepted them near Dera Baba Darshan Singh in Ghanupur Kale village while they were travelling on a motorcycle and a scooter. A search led to the seizure of 10 kg heroin, the DGP said.Further investigation is underway to establish the forward and backward linkages of the network and identify other persons involved in the smuggling operation, he added.A case under Sections 21, 25 and 29 of the NDPS Act has been registered at the State Special Operations Cell (SSOC) police station in Amritsar.    ower in premarket trading. The auto-parts supplier posted adjusted earnings of $0.19 a share on revenue of $970 million for the quarter ended Sept. 30, 2026, missing the $2.33 a share forecast by a wide margin while topping the $936.4 million revenue estimate. Shares fell 4.23% to $81.24 from the previous close of $84.83.Key Takeaways* Revenue rose 6% from a year earlier to $970 million, helped by new launches and customer recoveries.* Adjusted earnings missed expectations sharply because of $77 million in warranty charges tied to two instrument-cluster programs.* Excluding those charges, adjusted EBITDA would have been $123 million, above $119 million a year earlier.* Management cut full-year 2026 adjusted EBITDA guidance to $370 million to $410 million from $455 million to $495 million.* The company said it launched 21 new products in the quarter and remains on track for $6 billion in full-year new business bookings.Company PerformanceVisteon said it outperformed the broader auto production environment in the quarter, even as customer-weighted vehicle production fell about 5 percentage points. Sales increased 6% year over year, with growth supported by recent product launches, customer recoveries tied to electronic cost inflation and stronger performance in Europe and Asia outside China.Management said the quarter showed the company's ability to gain share in a difficult market. Europe was the strongest region, with sales growth of nearly 20%, while Asia excluding China grew about 14%. China remained weak, with customer production down about 17%, but Visteon said it is shifting more of its China business toward domestic automakers that are gaining share.The company also continued to expand into adjacent mobility markets, including two-wheelers and commercial vehicles, while moving more of its portfolio toward higher-value cockpit computing and display systems.Financial Highlights* Revenue: $970 million, up 6% year over year.* Adjusted earnings per share: $0.19, down sharply from the $2.33 forecast.* Revenue beat: $33.6 million above Wall Street expectations, or 3.6%.* Adjusted EBITDA: $46 million, or 4.7% of sales, including warranty charges.* Adjusted EBITDA excluding warranty charges: $123 million, or 12.7% of sales.* Warranty charges: $77 million related to two instrument-cluster programs.* Adjusted free cash flow: negative $1 million in the quarter.* Gross profit margin: 12.81% over the last twelve months, reflecting ongoing pressure from component costs.* Market capitalization: $2.18 billion at current share price.* Net cash: $136 million at quarter-end.* Customer recoveries for electronic cost inflation: about $150 million in the quarter.Earnings vs. ForecastVisteon's third-quarter results were mixed. Revenue beat estimates by 3.6%, but earnings fell far short of expectations. The company earned $0.19 a share, compared with the $2.33 forecast, a shortfall of $2.14 a share, or 91.8%.The size of the miss was unusually large relative to the revenue beat. That gap suggests the market focused less on sales growth and more on the hit to profitability from warranty charges and rising component costs. Management said the charges related to previously delivered products and that the underlying business remained strong.The company's adjusted EBITDA, excluding the warranty charges, was slightly above the prior-year quarter, which indicates the core operating trend was better than the headline EPS result suggests. Still, the earnings miss was large enough to overshadow the top-line beat.Market ReactionVisteon shares fell 4.23% to $81.24 in premarket trading, down $3.59 from the prior close of $84.83. The stock was trading near the bottom of its 52-week range of $77.01 to $126.95, leaving i                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              he Carbon variant at the national media ride in Jaipur to find out how these updates translate into the riding experience.Hero Xtreme 160R 4V Review - New Design And FeaturesThe Xtreme 160R has struggled to match the sales volumes of its established rivals, while the broader Apache and Pulsar families remain prominent in the 150cc to 200cc motorcycle segment. This update gives Hero a fresh opportunity to attract buyers, with a design closer to the larger Xtreme 250R and new technology to strengthen its appeal.In that direction, Xtreme 160R 4V gets the new CyberSharp design language that brings it closer to Xtreme 250R aesthetically. There's a new LED headlight design along with new LED DRL signature. These projector headlights also get auto brightness and then there is LED blinkers along with slightly revised LED tail lights.The fuel tank is all-new with a multi-layer approach with sporty and aggressive tank shrouds along with 3D XTREME badging. The new headlights get edgy detailing too and can be fitted with aero fins as accessories that look much sportier. Only the top-spec Carbon trim gets the carbon fiber looking parts on side body and engine cowl.Only the Carbon Red, Carbon Black and Carbon Grey colours get Red painted front wheel, while the Abrax Orange does not. There are stylish decals on rear body panels and the bike gets a tyre hugger and slightly reshaped seat. Rider section has been made narrower, while the pillion area has gotten wider.New Functionalities!With the 2027 Xtreme 160R 4V, Hero MotoCorp has added new functionalities too. For starters, we now get adjustable clutch and brake levers, which is appreciated for all sorts of hand sizes to access these levers. The illuminated switchgear is one of the most under-rated features that OEMs ignored in the past and are making a comeback. We're all for it.However, the main added functionality is the new 4.2-inch TFT instrument cluster that comes with a host of features. For starters, it has multiple trip computers and even a fuel efficiency computer. The unit I experienced showed 39 km/l which we don't know is accurate or not, as we didn't do a proper tank-to-tank mileage test. There is no document storage with this cluster, but quarter mile drag time and 0-60 km/h acceleration timer are present.The cluster has automatic Day / Night themes along with auto brightness adjustment. The screen has a matte anti-glare coating and was legible under direct sunlight. The auto theme switching and auto brightness worked seamlessly without any issues. With this update, Hero has added panic braking alert where blinkers flash automatically to alert other road users. Also, Ride modes - Rain, Road and Sport, with their own colour themes.When connected to Bluetooth, it will allow music controls, call and message alerts, and phone battery status. If you were expecting Google Maps mirroring, you'd be disappointed because both of its immediate rivals offer this feature now. Also, only the owners whose phone numbers are registered with the bike in the server, can get the OTP and connect to the cluster. So, I couldn't access any Bluetooth functions and I wish it wasn't the case.Powertrains, Ride, DynamicsUpdated Hero Xtreme 160R 4V comes with the same engine as before, a 163.2cc single-cylinder unit with oil-cooling and a 4V head. With the update, Hero has bumped the peak power slightly, which now outputs at 17.1 PS, up from 16.8 PS at 8,750 RPM, while torque remains the same at 14.6 Nm, mated to an electronic throttle and a 5-speed gearbox.The clutch was light to operate and the engine is mostly vibe-free if you're not close to the red line. Even the throttle                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     ‹ ‹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              î>Å!5Å'-Ç/ §%95150826742026-10-22T13:59:52ZTiger Shroff: Anil Pandit wanted in firing case, four in police custodyDeccan Heralddeccanherald.comhttps://www.deccanherald.com/india/maharashtra/tiger-shroff-firing-case-gangster-anil-pandit-named-as-wanted-accused-4-get-police-custody-4191581engIndiaø’UUUUUWMumbai: Gangster Anil Pandit who had claimed responsibility for the firing on Bollywood actor Tiger Shroff has been named as a wanted accused in the case, police told a court on Thursday as it remanded four arrested persons in police custody till October 26.. Pandit, a member of the Lawrence Bishnoi gang who is said to be operating from the US, had allegedly claimed responsibility for the October 18 attack on Shroff in a social media post.. The Crime Branch of Mumbai Police on Thursday produced Kunal (21) and Sumit (19), both hailing from Haryana's Sonipat district, and Radha (23) and Babita (23), both from Uttar Pradesh, before the Esplanade Magistrate's court here.. The firing on Shroff was carried out at Pandit's instructions and he has been named as a wanted accused in the case, the police told the court..'Put Lawrence Bishnoi in anda-cell of Arthur Road Jail': Opposition parties to Maharashtra govt after Tiger Shroff attack. The investigators sought police custody of the four arrested accused, stating that they had actively participated in the conspiracy with those held earlier in the case.. These four accused had supplied a weapon to the shooters, the police said.. Kunal and Sumit collected a pistol and 20 rounds from Amritsar on Anil Pandit's instructions, while Radha and Babita travelled to Mumbai and handed over the weapon to other conspirators, the police said.. Defence lawyers Ajinkya Mirgul and Satyavrat Joshi opposed the demand for custody, arguing that there was no material against these four accused and they had been arrested on mere suspicion.. After hearing both sides, the court remanded the four accused in police custody till October 26.. At least two men fired shots at Tiger Shroff's car in Mumbai's Juhu area on the evening of October 18 before fleeing on a motorcycle. The actor escaped relatively unhurt, while his driver and a bystander were injured.. The motorcycle was later found abandoned in suburban Andheri.. Mumbai Police have registered a case of attempt to murder and arrested a total of 10 persons so far.. On Wednesday, the court remanded Rehan alias Sukka, from Sonipat in Haryana; Ritik Kurmi alias Roshan, originally from Begusarai in Bihar and living in Mumbai; and Phool Singh from Haryana in police custody till October 31.°`Å!5Å/7Ç' æ[95150933452026-10-22T14:06:29ZUltraviolette Automotive bets big on new models to drive global expansionBusiness Standardbusiness-standard.comhttps://www.business-standard.com/industry/auto/ultraviolette-automotive-bets-big-on-new-models-to-drive-global-expansion-126102201415_1.htmlengIndia?π Ultraviolette Automotive aims to generate 20-25% of its vehicle sales from exports over the next two to three years as it expands into global markets.| Image: Company's Faceboo   o†TÅ!5Å%Q æM95151051622026-10-22T14:14:49ZNew Hero Xtreme 160R 4V Review - Can It Take On Apache And Pulsar?RushLanerushlane.comhttps://www.rushlane.com/?p=559119engIndia?””””””’Hero MotoCorp has launched the updated Xtreme 160R 4V with sharper styling, more features and a small increase in power, aiming to strengthen its presence in the competitive sporty 160cc segment. Prices start at Rs 1.27 lakh and rise to Rs 1.41 lakh for the top-spec Carbon variant, both ex-showroom. I rode t   m    k ProfileElectric motorcycle maker Ultraviolette Automotive, which is planning to come up with a new 250,000-unit facility in Tamil Nadu's Hosur, on Thursday said it is targeting exports to contribute around 20-25 per cent to its total vehicle sales over the next two to three years. The company is targeting markets like Europe, North America, and Latin America for exports.The company said this while introducing VX-100, its new 100V platform. Alongside the platform, it launched the X-47 Venom, which is the next generation of its X-47 Crossover and the first motorcycle built on this new architecture. The company is preparing to introduce four products over the next year on its new VX-100 electric vehicle platform, including the much-awaited Shockwave and Tesseract, alongside other motorcycles and scooters.According to reports, bookings have already started for the X-47 Venom, and it will be sold at an introductory price of ‚Çπ2.65 lakh for the first 5,000 units, with the regular starting price set at ‚Çπ3 lakh. The motorcycle also establishes a new benchmark in electric two-wheeler (e2W) charging, with the capability to support 12 kilowatt (kW) DC fast charging.The Bengaluru-based company will initially focus on around 20 European countries -- including Germany, France, Italy, Spain, the Netherlands, and the UK -- with its X-47 Venom motorcycle set to make its European debut at the EICMA motorcycle show in Milan on November 3."We are getting big on exports. Over the next two to three years, around 20-25 per cent of our entire vehicle sales will come from international markets. Europe, North America, and Latin America will be our focus markets," Niraj Rajmohan, cofounder and chief technology officer of Ultraviolette Automotive, told Business Standard.Also ReadEV supply struggles to keep pace with surging demand during festive seasonpremiumStellantis India aims to halve vehicle development time, boost localisationBS-VII clock starts ticking as Centre sets 2029 target for emission normsHyundai replicating India learnings across global markets: CEO Jose Mu√±ozShipping disruptions may make price hikes inevitable: Volvo Cars India MDThe company is investing around ‚Çπ800 crore in the Hosur unit, and it will add to the company's existing facility near Bengaluru, which can produce up to 50,000 units annually. Rajmohan said the Hosur unit will include multiple vehicle assembly lines and battery production lines, with battery manufacturing capacity too."We expect our demand to pick up once the new products are launched. We hope to sell around 20,000 vehicles a month, comprising around 10,000 scooters and 10,000 motorcycles within two years. In fact, monthly demand may cross 10,000 vehicles by the end of the next financial year," he added.The X-47 Venom represents the next generation of the X-47 Crossover, combining enhanced performance, improved energy utilisation, faster charging, and advanced rider intelligence in a single package. With an Indian Driving Cycle (IDC) range of 343 km, peak power of 30 kW (40 hp), torque of 550 Nm (at the wheel), and a top speed of 155 kmph, the X-47 Venom is engineered for riders seeking high performance without compromising everyday usability.The motorcycle accelerates from 0-100 kmph in 5.9 seconds, and has a kerb weight of 180 kg, making it up to 20 kg lighter than the previous generation. The motorcycle also offers cruise control at the push of a button for added convenience during sustained highway riding. It also continues to feature UV Hypersense (Radar Intelligence), reinforcing Ultraviolette's focus on bringing next-generation rider technology to the two-wheeler segment.Automakers urge Centre to defer BS-VII brake and tyre norms until 2035premiumIndia's auto tech funding at $1.3 bn in 2026, unlike                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        „ ∂≈‘„                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   †pÅ	!5ÅAÇ- º95150683252026-10-22T13:47:34ZTiger Shroff's team posts video of red marks on his arms amid ongoing firing investigationIndia News, Breaking News, Entertainment News | India.comindia.comhttps://www.india.com/entertainment/bollywood/tiger-shroffs-team-posts-video-of-red-marks-on-his-arms-amid-ongoing-firing-investigation-8538219/engIndiaø∑The recent post has drawn attention as investigators continue to probe the attack. An alleged Lawrence Bishnoi gang operative, A   s°eÅ!5Åw[!ÅQ æM95150684722026-10-22T13:47:41ZKinetic Watts MD Ajinkya Firodia: Electric 2 Wheeler Industry Needs A Standardised Charging Interface Like USB Type CCarToq - India‚Äôs #1 auto content sitecartoq.comhttps://www.cartoq.com/bike-news/kinetic-watts-expands-electric-two-wheeler-portfolio-dx-capacity/engIndia?∑Mr. Ajinkya Firodia, Vice Chairman and Managing Director of Kinetic Watts and Volts Ltd, in conversation with Jayaprashanth Mohanram, Editor at CarToq.Jayaprashan   r°@Å %5Å?5'Å[ æMb-95150690462026-10-22T13:48:09ZUltraviolette VX100 Platform to Power 4 New EV Models in 12 Months | Autocar ProfessionalAutocar Professionalautocarpro.inhttps://www.autocarpro.in/news/ultraviolette-plans-four-vx100-based-products-over-next-12-months-135406engIndia?ªThe 100V platform offers up to 25% higher battery energy density, twice the cooling performance and faster charging, while reducing vehicle weight.Bengaluru-based electric two-wheeler manufacturer Ul   qéFÅ!5Å;#-Åw òQ95150823412026-10-22T13:59:42ZRohit Godara gang member arrested for firing at Ludhiana visa consultancy - The TribuneThe Tribunetribuneindia.comhttps://www.tribuneindia.com/news/ludhiana/rohit-godara-gang-member-arrested-for-firing-at-ludhiana-visa-consultancy/engIndiaøŒûûûûûûThe Ludhiana police have arrested one of the two men accused of opening fire at a visa consultant's office in Model Town Extension.The arrested shooter has been identified as Deepak Kumar, a resident of Salem Tabri.The police investigation into the high-profile case revealed that Deepak was allegedly a member of the gang operated by wanted overseas gangster Rohit Godara.Deepak, along with another alleged gang member, identified as Gagandeep Singh of Delhi, allegedly opened fire at the office in Model Town Extension on the directions of two key members of the Rohit Godara gang, Varinder Charan of Rajasthan and Jagjit Singh Gandhi of Ludhiana, who are also believed to be operating from abroad.According to the police, Varinder Charan handles Rohit Godara's operations in India.In the latest case, Charan allegedly called the owner of the visa consultancy office and demanded Rs 1 crore. When the demand was not met, he allegedly sent Deepak and Gagandeep to open fire at the office of Brightway Immigration to intimidate its owner. The incident took place on the night of October 10-11.ADCP Crime Rupinder Bhatti told The Tribune the police also recovered the black Hero Splendor motorcycle, which allegedly had no registration number plate and was used in the crime.Deepak was produced before a competent court, which remanded him to police custody. Raids are underway at the suspected hideouts of his absconding associate, Gagandeep. The police also said that the illegal weapon used in the firing had been supplied by a member of the Godara gang.    traviolette Automotive plans to introduce four products based on its newly developed VX100 platform over the next 12 months, as it expands its portfolio beyond premium electric motorcycles into scooters and more affordable motorcycle segments.The company's new 100V architecture made its production debut on the X-47 Venom, launched on Thursday at an introductory price of Rs 2.65 lakh. Ultraviolette is preparing to introduce the Tesseract electric scooter and Shockwave motorcycle in the first quarter of 2027, while an updated version of its F77 sports motorcycle is also under development."So, the next one year, you will see four new products coming in with the same VX100," Niraj Rajmohan, Co-founder and Chief Technology Officer of Ultraviolette Automotive, said on the sidelines of the X-47 Venom launch.Rajmohan did not specify all four products or clarify whether the newly launched X-47 Venom was included in the count.He said the new platform would eventually be extended across the company's product portfolio, allowing different motorcycles and scooters to benefit from a common technology architecture."We haven't made an announcement about the F77. But it's in the works," Rajmohan said, without specifying a launch timeline.The VX100 platform incorporates a new battery pack, electric motor, motor controller and vehicle electronics. According to Ultraviolette, the 100V architecture offers 25% higher battery energy density, improved thermal management and faster charging, while reducing the weight of key powertrain components.The X-47 Venom, the first model based on the architecture, is offered with 7.5 kWh and 11 kWh battery packs, delivering claimed Indian Driving Cycle (IDC) ranges of 227 km and 343 km, respectively. The maximum range compares with 323 km on the previous X-47 Crossover. Both new versions produce 30 kW (40 hp), have a claimed top speed of 155 kmph and can accelerate from zero to 100 kmph in 5.9 seconds.The new X-47 Venom weighs 180 kg in its 7.5 kWh configuration and 195 kg with the 11 kWh battery, compared with 197 kg and 208 kg, respectively, for the corresponding previous-generation versions. This represents weight reductions of 17 kg and 13 kg. The company also claims the new platform supports external DC fast charging at up to 12 kW, with deliveries scheduled to begin in November. Its regular starting price is Rs 3 lakh.The company selected the X-47 as the first model to receive the platform because the crossover motorcycle has emerged as its largest-selling product."We've seen this, 90% of our volumes have become the X47," Rajmohan said, adding that the model attracts buyers across street, sports and adventure motorcycle segments.Ultraviolette currently sells the F77 performance motorcycle and X-47 Crossover, including their variants. Its upcoming Shockwave is positioned as a lightweight motorcycle for urban commuting and recreational riding, while the Tesseract will mark the company's entry into the electric scooter market.The Shockwave was earlier announced at Rs 1.75 lakh and the Tesseract at Rs 1.45 lakh. Both products have been redesigned around the company's 100V architecture, with their commercial introductions scheduled for the first quarter of 2027.The Tesseract's launch was previously pushed back after the shift to the new architecture required changes to its battery, controller, battery management and charging systems.The product expansion comes as Ultraviolette seeks to build on strong sales growth in 2026, although its volumes remain relatively small compared with established electric two-wheeler manufacturers.According to VAHAN retail registration data, Ultraviolette sold 3,200 electric motorcycles between January 1 and August 12, 2026, compared with 1,559 units duri                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         th Mohanram: Kinetic Watts resurrected the legendary Kinetic two stroke scooter in electric form through the DX. While a lot of people born in the 80s and 90s will immediately connect with the design out of sheer nostalgia, the younger folks - read millennials - are now used to sharper design language. So, will Kinetic bring in differently designed electric scooters in the near future? If so, can you share a timeline, and possibly the direction you're going to take with future products.Ajinkya Firodia: The Kinetic DX represents a legacy that holds a special place in the hearts of millions of Indians, while bringing that familiar design into a contemporary electric mobility experience. With the DX, our intention was to bring back an iconic design in a way that feels relevant to today's consumers, combining familiarity with modern technology, convenience and performance.The DX is designed for the family scooter segment, which accounts for around 70% of the scooter segment. This makes it a natural fit for the needs of Indian families, across both rural and urban markets. Going forward, we envisage several variants with different battery capacities and price points, allowing us to address a wider range of customer needs while building on the family scooter proposition.At Kinetic, we also recognise that consumer preferences are changing and that different generations look for different things from their mobility choices. As we expand our electric two-wheeler portfolio, we will continue to listen to consumers and understand these changing needs. This could mean exploring different design approaches and products for different use cases, while staying true to the values that have always been associated with Kinetic - practicality, reliability and accessibility.The DX is an important part of our journey, but it is only the beginning. We are working towards building a wider portfolio over time. While we are not in a position to share specific timelines or product details at this stage, our focus will be on bringing products that combine distinctive design, relevant technology and a strong understanding of what Indian consumers want.Jayaprashanth Mohanram: One thing I was impressed about the Kinetic DX was the built-in charger. What prompted you to take this step? Also, do you expect other manufacturers to follow this idea?Ajinkya Firodia: Kinetic has always stood for innovation that makes everyday mobility simpler. The original DX removed pain points such as kicking and gear shifting, and with the new DX, we wanted to follow the same philosophy - identify the everyday pain points of EV ownership and find practical ways to address them.One of those pain points is having to carry, remove and store a separate charger. This led us to develop the integrated charging solution with a retractable cable, making charging more convenient and seamless for the customer. The retractable cable technology is globally patented by Kinetic. While other manufacturers may explore built-in charging solutions, the pull-out cable is what gives the DX its added ease and convenience.For us, the focus is not about adding features for the sake of it, but about understanding what makes EV ownership easier and more practical. The Easy Flip feature follows the same philosophy - reducing everyday effort through thoughtful innovation.Whether other manufacturers adopt a similar approach will depend on their product philosophy and customer requirements. What is important is that the industry continues to innovate around the real needs of consumers and makes electric mobility simpler and more accessible.Jayaprashanth Mohanram: Cars have a unified charging port interface through the CCS2 adapter. While I understand that this requires hardware that could prove to be relatively expensive for a                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     nil Pandit, has also been named as a wanted accused in the case.Actor Tiger Shroff's team has shared a video showing red marks on his arms, days after gunshots were fired at his car in Mumbai's Juhu area. The video was posted on Thursday, October 22, on the team's official Instagram page, Team Tiger Shroff, with the caption, "God is kind." The post comes as Mumbai Police continue to investigate the firing incident that took place on Sunday evening. While Tiger escaped direct gunshot injuries, his driver and a bystander were injured in the incident. The case has also raised concerns about the safety of several Bollywood celebrities.Tiger Shroff's team posts video days after shooting incidentThe short video shared by Tiger's team shows several small, dot-like marks and red spots across the actor's arm. The post did not provide any further explanation about the marks or clarify whether they were connected to the firing incident.The video was shared days after two men allegedly opened fire at Tiger's car before fleeing on a motorcycle. The actor had reportedly been leaving a football ground in Juhu when the incident occurred. Although Tiger escaped direct gunshot injuries, his driver sustained a bullet wound and a bystander was also injured.See viral video of Tiger Shroff hereTen people arrested so far in Tiger Shroff firing caseMumbai Police have arrested 10 people in connection with the case so far. Three accused, identified as Rehan alias Sukka from Sonipat in Haryana, Ritik Kurmi alias Roshan, originally from Begusarai in Bihar and residing in Mumbai, and Phool Singh from Haryana, were detained at Kota railway station in Rajasthan.Investigators are examining the alleged recruitment network, the movement of weapons and suspected links between the accused and the Lawrence Bishnoi gang. The investigation is also looking into who allegedly planned the attack and how it was carried out.The Lawrence Bishnoi gang and the actual targetGangster Anil Pandit, an alleged operative of the Lawrence Bishnoi gang reportedly based in the United States, has been named as a wanted accused in the case. On Thursday, Mumbai Police told the Esplanade court that the firing was allegedly carried out at Pandit's behest. A native of Panipat in Haryana, he is suspected of operating a criminal network from the US and coordinating with shooters in India.An audio clip that surfaced earlier featured a man identifying himself as Pandit and claiming responsibility for the attack on Tiger. The speaker also allegedly threatened people associated with Salman Khan.In the purported recording, the man claimed that Tiger had been warned against working with Salman, suggesting that their professional association was behind the attack. He also allegedly threatened members of the film industry who work with Salman and those who speak against the Bishnoi gang. Meanwhile, reports have suggested that Salman Khan, rather than Tiger Shroff, was the intended target.The possibility has raised serious concerns about the security of the Bollywood superstar. Following the incident, security has reportedly been tightened around Salman's Bandra residence, with additional precautions also being taken to ensure his safety during public appearances.Shooters allegedly conducted recce at other Bollywood stars' housesAs per NDTV reports, the investigation has also brought the security of other Bollywood celebrities into focus. Shooters allegedly conducted reconnaissance at the residences of Aamir Khan, Akshay Kumar, Amitabh Bachchan and Ranbir Kapoor. The reported development has raised further concerns about the safety of prominent film personalities amid the ongoing investigation.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        o o                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     ïÅ!5Å'=Ç •C95150562922026-10-22T13:38:26ZUltraviolette launches X-47 Venom at ‚Çπ2.65 lakh, deliveries in November@businesslinethehindubusinessline.comhttps://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/ultraviolette-launches-x-47-venom-at-265-lakh-deliveries-in-november/article71611335.eceengIndia?±Electric two-wheeler maker Ultraviolette Automotive on Thursday launched the X-47 Venom, the first motorcycle built on its new 100V VX-100 architecture, as it gears up to roll out the Tesseract and Shockwave models next year.Priced at an introductory ‚Çπ2.65 lakh, the new motorcycle will be available in India from November, with the company also planning a European launch at the EICMA motorcycle show in Milan on November 3.The next-generation X-47 Crossover comes with an IDC-certified range of 343 km, a top speed of 155 kmph and a 0-100 kmph acceleration time of 5.9 seconds. It delivers 40 hp of power and 550 Nm of torque at the wheel. The motorcycle is 20 kg lighter than the previous-generation platform, with the company introducing a new LIGHTFURY motor and SRB-11 battery technology.The VX-100 architecture is designed to improve performance, efficiency, and charging speeds. The X-47 Venom supports 12 kW DC fast charging, which the company claims is a first for an Indian electric two-wheeler. It also features a 1.6 kW onboard charger.On the technology front, the motorcycle features a seven-inch touchscreen, radar-based rider assistance, integrated front and rear dashcams, cruise control, and a battery management system with a pyro-fuse. The latter is designed to disconnect the battery in the event of a critical failure. Ultraviolette also unveiled B-MAX, an airbag-equipped riding vest intended to protect the rider's upper body in a crash.Speaking to businessline ahead of the launch, Niraj Rajmohan, CTO and Co-Founder, Ultraviolette, said the company planned to launch about four vehicles on the VX-100 platform over the next year, with the X-47 Venom being the first. The X-47 retains its crossover positioning, targeting riders looking for a motorcycle suited to city commutes, long-distance rides and occasional off-road use, he added.The company is targeting monthly production of 1,000 vehicles and expects volumes across its portfolio to reach 10,000 units a month by October 2027. Exports currently account for more than 10 per cent of its volumes and are expected to rise to 15 per cent over the next year and 20-25 per cent in two to two-and-a-half years, Rajmohan said.Alongside the VX-100 architecture, X-47 Venom, and UV LIGHTFURY motor, Ultraviolette is introducing B-MAX, a new reusable safety vest for riders.Published on October 22, 2026    ó  ó                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             êeÅ!5ÅÅY ùi95150526092026-10-22T13:35:24ZFarmer dies after car in Erode Collector's convoy hits two-wheelerdtNext.indtnext.inhttps://www.dtnext.in/news/tamilnadu/farmer-dies-after-car-in-erode-collectors-convoy-hits-two-wheelerengIndiaø–PPPPPPCHENNAI: A 50-year-old farmer died after a car in Erode District Collector Kandasamy's convoy collided with his two-wheeler on the Gobi-Erode highway on Thursday. The accident occurred as the man was returning home after renewing his driving licence at the Gobichettipalayam Regional Transport Office (RTO), according to a Maalaimalar report..The deceased was identified as Shanmugam, a resident of Vellangoil near Gobichettipalayam in Erode district. He had visited the RTO earlier in the day to complete the licence renewal process and was travelling home on his two-wheeler when the collision occurred..Car in Collector's convoy hits two-wheelerAccording to the report, Shanmugam was attempting to cross the highway when a car travelling towards Erode as part of the District Collector's convoy struck his vehicle. The impact threw him off the two-wheeler, leaving him with severe injuries. The accident took place while the convoy was proceeding towards Erode. The collision drew the attention of people in the vicinity, while security personnel accompanying the convoy also rushed to help the injured farmer..Farmer rushed to Gobichettipalayam hospitalBystanders and security personnel immediately took Shanmugam to the Gobichettipalayam Government Hospital for emergency treatment. However, after examining him, doctors declared that he had died on the way to the hospital. The incident has caused grief in Vellangoil and the surrounding areas, particularly as Shanmugam had been returning from the RTO after completing an official task when the accident occurred..Police begin investigation into accidentPolice have registered a case and begun an investigation into the circumstances leading to the collision. Investigators are expected to examine the sequence of events, including how the car and the two-wheeler came into contact while Shanmugam was crossing the highway.   ë Aë                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     á,Å!5ÅK!Ç) âq95150505322026-10-22T13:33:57ZTiger Shroff's Team Shares Video Of His Injury After Firing Attack Amid Trolling | "God Is KindNews18news18.comhttps://www.news18.com/videos/india/tiger-shroff-s-team-shares-video-of-his-injury-after-firing-attack-amid-trolling-god-is-kind-10393338.htmlengIndiaøîActor Tiger Shroff's team released a video of his injured arms on Thursday, days after the Bollywood star narrowly survived a gun attack on his car.At least two men fired gunshots at Shroff's car in Mumbai's Juhu area on Sunday evening before running off on a motorcycle. The actor managed to escape relatively unhurt, but his driver and a bystander were injured.Team Tiger Shroff, the official Instagram page run by his team, shared the video showing the actor's injured hands. Sharing the video, the team penned in the caption, "God is kind." n18oc_indian18oc_breaking-newsNews18 Mobile App - https://onelink.to/desc-youtubeì;Å%5ÅE+/Ç ¢b-95150508732026-10-22T13:34:09ZTiger Shroff firing case: Gangster Anil Pandit named as wanted accused; 4 get police custodyMillennium Postmillenniumpost.inhttps://www.millenniumpost.in/nation/tiger-shroff-firing-case-gangster-anil-pandit-named-as-wanted-accused-4-get-police-custody-680596engIndiaø‚ÚÚÚÚÚÛMumbai: Gangster Anil Pandit who had claimed responsibility for the firing on Bollywood actor Tiger Shroff has been named as a wanted accused in the case, police told a court on Thursday as it remanded four arrested persons in police custody till October 26.Pandit, a member of the Lawrence Bishnoi gang who is said to be operating from the US, had allegedly claimed responsibility for the October 18 attack on Shroff in a social media post.The Crime Branch of Mumbai Police on Thursday produced Kunal (21) and Sumit (19), both hailing from Haryana's Sonipat district, and Radha (23) and Babita (23), both from Uttar Pradesh, before the Esplanade Magistrate's court here.The firing on Shroff was carried out at Pandit's instructions and he has been named as a wanted accused in the case, the police told the court.The investigators sought police custody of the four arrested accused, stating that they had actively participated in the conspiracy with those held earlier in the case.These four accused had supplied weapons to the shooters, the police said.Kunal and Summit collected a pistol and 20 rounds from Amritsar on Anil Pandit's instructions, while Radha and Babita travelled to Mumbai and handed over the weapon to other conspirators, the police said.Defence lawyers Ajinkya Mirgul and Satyavrat Joshi opposed the demand for custody, arguing that there was no material against these four accused and they had been arrested on mere suspicion.After hearing both sides, the court remanded the four accused in police custody till October 26.At least two men fired shots at Tiger Shroff's car in Mumbai's Juhu area on the evening of October 18 before fleeing on a motorcycle. The actor escaped relatively unhurt, while his driver and a bystander were injured.The motorcycle was later found abandoned in suburban Andheri.Mumbai Police have registered a case of attempt to murder and arrested a total of 10 persons so far.On Wednesday, the court remanded Rehan alias Sukka, from Sonipat in Haryana; Ritik Kurmi alias Roshan, originally from Begusarai in Bihar and living in Mumbai; and Phool Singh from Haryana in police custody till October 31.   d ád                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        íÅ!5Å!Åe †a95150463352026-10-22T13:31:23ZBengaluru police crack woman's murder case, husband arrestedNews18news18.comhttps://www.news18.com/agency-feeds/bengaluru-police-crack-womans-murder-case-husband-arrested-10393327.htmlengIndiaø◊◊◊◊◊◊◊Bengaluru, Oct 22 (PTI) A man was arrested for allegedly murdering his 25-year-old wife over suspicion of an illicit relationship and dumping her body in a stormwater drain, police said on Thursday.The decomposed body of Ruksana, a native of Bihar, was found in a stormwater drain within the Magadi Road police station limits on October 13. Police said she was allegedly killed by her husband, Mohammed Irfaz, on October 10.According to police, Irfaz allegedly hit Ruksana on the head with a stone during an argument. He then placed her body in a bag and dumped it in the drain.The couple had married about a year ago and moved to Bengaluru around six months ago, where they worked in garment factories, police said.Ruksana, who had a child from her previous marriage, had met Irfaz in Bihar after separating from her first husband, and the two subsequently fell in love, according to police.Police said Irfaz allegedly suspected his wife of having an illicit relationship because she frequently spoke to people over the phone, which led to regular arguments between them.On October 10, an argument allegedly escalated, following which Irfaz attacked her with a stone, police said.To dispose of the body, the accused allegedly broke the handle lock of a motorcycle belonging to an unidentified person, placed the body inside a bag on the vehicle and pushed it before dumping the body in the drain, police said.After the body was discovered on October 13, police formed a special team to establish the woman's identity and trace the accused.During the investigation, police received information from local residents that a man from Gopalapura had reported his wife missing.When questioned, Irfaz allegedly told police that Ruksana had returned to her native place in Bihar and was not using a mobile phone. However, suspecting inconsistencies in his account, police took him into custody for further questioning.During interrogation, he allegedly confessed to the murder, police said.The Magadi Road police are continuing their investigation into the case. PTI KSU KHäuÅ!5Å'-Ç ë/95150481312026-10-22T13:32:46ZOne dead, another seriously injured in road accident at Bengal's HooghlyThe Statesmanthestatesman.comhttps://www.thestatesman.com/cities/kolkata/one-dead-another-seriously-injured-in-road-accident-at-bengals-hooghly-1503653887.htmlengIndiaø∆ñññññöA civil engineer riding pilion was killed as a speeding pickup van lost control and collided head-on with their motorcycle. The incident took place on Dashami, Wednesday evening at the junction of Khamargachhi and Muktarpur roads in Bengal's Hooghly district.The deceased identified as Bikram Biswas, was a resident of the Dakshin Milangarh area in Hooghly's Balagarh. He was employed in a firm at Bengaluru and had returned home to spend the Puja holidays with his family.According to his relative, Biswajit Biswas, on Dashami evening, Bikram set out on his motorcycle with a friend riding pillion to buy something. When they reached the junction, the pickup van allegedly lost control and collided head-on with their motorcycle. Both riders were thrown off the vehicle and seriously injured.Police rushed both victims to Jirat Shyamaprasad Gramin Hospital, where the attending doctor declared Bikram brought dead. His seriously injured friend was subsequently shifted to the Imambara District Hospital in Chinsurah.The deceased's wife was worried about the family's livelihood in the days ahead.   ≤ ≤                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        îJÅ!5Å95Ç §I95150459002026-10-22T13:31:02ZMumbai police name Anil Pandit wanted accused in Tiger Shroff firing caseThe New Indian Expressnewindianexpress.comhttps://www.newindianexpress.com/india/2026/Oct/22/mumbai-police-name-anil-pandit-wanted-accused-in-tiger-shroff-firing-caseengIndiaø„333333Gangster Anil Pandit, who allegedly claimed responsibility for the October 18 firing incident involving Bollywood actor Tiger Shroff, has been named a wanted accused in the case, police told a court on Thursday. The court remanded four arrested persons in police custody till October 26.Pandit, a member of the Lawrence Bishnoi gang who is believed to be operating from the US, allegedly claimed responsibility for the attack on Shroff in a social media post.The Crime Branch of Mumbai Police on Thursday produced Kunal (21) and Sumit (19), both hailing from Haryana's Sonipat district, and Radha (23) and Babita (23), both from Uttar Pradesh, before the Esplanade Magistrate's court here.The firing on Shroff was carried out at Pandit's instructions and he has been named as a wanted accused in the case, the police told the court.The investigators sought police custody of the four arrested accused, stating that they had actively participated in the conspiracy with those held earlier in the case..Three accused in Tiger Shroff car firing case brought to Mumbai; four others held.These four accused had supplied weapons to the shooters, the police said.Kunal and Summit collected a pistol and 20 rounds from Amritsar on Anil Pandit's instructions, while Radha and Babita travelled to Mumbai and handed over the weapon to other conspirators, the police said.Defence lawyers Ajinkya Mirgul and Satyavrat Joshi opposed the demand for custody, arguing that there was no material against these four accused and they had been arrested on mere suspicion.After hearing both sides, the court remanded the four accused in police custody till October 26.At least two men fired shots at Tiger Shroff's car in Mumbai's Juhu area on the evening of October 18 before fleeing on a motorcycle. The actor escaped relatively unhurt, while his driver and a bystander were injured.The motorcycle was later found abandoned in suburban Andheri.Mumbai Police have registered a case of attempt to murder and arrested a total of 10 persons so far.On Wednesday, the court remanded Rehan alias Sukka, from Sonipat in Haryana; Ritik Kurmi alias Roshan, originally from Begusarai in Bihar and living in Mumbai; and Phool Singh from Haryana in police custody till October 31.Download the TNIE app to stay with us and follow the latest   ± ¢±                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     †IÅ!5Å-''Ç+ º395150420712026-10-22T13:28:20ZBREAKING: Ultraviolette X47 Venom Launched; Here Is Everything You Need To Know:ZigWheels.comzigwheels.comhttps://www.zigwheels.com/news-features/launch-story/breaking-ultraviolette-x47-venom-launched-with-an-introductory-price-of-rs-265-lakh/58372/engIndia?»òòòòòó* X47 Venom launched at Rs 2.65 lakh (introductory ex-showroom)* Gets a new battery architecture* Packed with all-new tech and featuresThe Bengaluru-based EV two-wheeler man   zîZÅ!5ÅQ!ÇE §+95150445372026-10-22T13:29:52ZTiger Shroff's Team Shares Video Of Bruises And Pellet Marks Days After Firing Attack On The ActorNews18news18.comhttps://www.news18.com/movies/bollywood/tiger-shroffs-team-shares-video-of-bruises-and-pellet-marks-days-after-firing-attack-on-the-actor-ws-l-10393310.htmlengIndiaø∆ñññññöTiger Shroff's team has shared a video showing bruises and pellet marks on his arm after the October 18 firing incident in Mumbai, as the police investigation continues.Tiger Shroff was attacked in Mumbai on October 18, when unidentified men opened fire at his car while he was returning home from a football match. Now, the actor's team has released a video featuring bruises and pellet marks after the attack, as the investigation into the case continues.The video shared by the team features a clip of Tiger's arm covered in bruises and pellet marks. The caption of the video reads, "God is kind üôèüèª"Tiger Shroff's Shooting Incident - All About ItThe firing took place near Pushpa Park in Juhu on Sunday, October 18. The actor had reportedly gone to play football and was returning home when the incident took place. According to initial reports, people had gathered in the area to catch a glimpse of the actor when the assailants allegedly opened fire. The other person injured in the incident was an autorickshaw driver, who was taken to Cooper Hospital. A day after the incident, police reportedly arrested the three unidentified men in Kota.A new twist emerged in the Tiger Shroff firing case, with Lokmat reporting, citing Mumbai Police sources, that the actor may not have been the assailants' original target. During preliminary interrogation, the accused allegedly revealed that they had travelled to Mumbai to target another prominent person. However, after reportedly struggling to reach their intended target, they allegedly shifted their focus to Tiger Shroff.The accused reportedly learned through Instagram reels posted by Tiger Shroff's fans that the actor did not usually have police security around him, making him an easier target. According to sources, the attackers allegedly planned to kill the actor with the intention of spreading fear in Bollywood.The Mumbai Crime Branch told a court on Wednesday that the firing at Shroff's car was allegedly carried out at the behest of Anil Pandit, an alleged associate of the Lawrence Bishnoi gang. The accused were reportedly traced through CCTV footage and the tracking of a motorcycle allegedly used in the incident. Defence lawyers opposed police custody, arguing that the accused had already been questioned during transit remand.    ufacturer has launched X47 Venom - a thorough update for the electric crossover bike. It has been launched at an introductory price of Rs 2,65,000 (ex-showroom, Bengaluru). Here is everything you need to know about the motorcycle:The GazeThe X47 Venom looks the same as the X47 and shares the same chassis and underpinnings. The motorcycle gets a rugged design with knuckle guards, a tall-ish windvisor and a sharp side profile, giving us a proper cross-over package.The bike comes in 4 unique shades: Stellar White, Turbo Red, Afterburner Yellow, and Cosmic Black, depending on the variant you pickBattery Pack & PerformanceThe X47 Venom is the first bike to be based on the brand's new VX-100 platform. It's a 100-volt architecture that has enabled a host of new updates, right from 12 kW fast charging. It has also allowed the usage of a 25% more energy dense battery pack that is 15% lighter, and increases the range. It has improved cooling - 2x faster cooling as per the brand.There's a new controller that allows the most performance-rich Ballistic riding mode to be used from 100% to 10% battery state-of-charge, unlike up to 30% previously. The battery regen can also now be enabled right from 99% battery SoC, unlike 90% previously. Ultraviolette has added a Pyro Fuse in the battery pack that can very quickly isolate a row of batteries as soon as an electrical anomaly is detected, keeping the bike safe.The new motor is 25% lighter, more efficient, and is claimed to have a sustained power delivery even at higher revs. The X47 Venom makes 30 kW and 90 Nm (peak), it gets a claimed top speed of 155 km and an IDC (Indian Driving Conditions) range of 345 km. It zips from 0-60 kmph in 2.7 seconds and 0-100 kmph in 5.9 seconds (claimed).Overall, the bike weighs almost 20 kg less than the outgoing model, its kerb weight is 180 kg. Also, it gets a seat height of 820 mm and a ground clearance of 200 mm.Feature-Packed To BrimThe bike gets an all-new 7-inch touch-enabled TFT display and comes with connectivity features such as Bluetooth connectivity, call/SMS alerts and navigation. It also gets cruise control (optional on base variant), which comes in handy while clocking on miles on the highway. Also, it gets dash cameras on the front and the rear that can record your rides in a loop, at 1080P 30FPS. These cameras are also used for park assist, help back in to or out of a parking space which enables parking assist that comes to rescue while navigating through tight parking spaces.The bike gets a radar at the rear, which enables features such as lane keep assist and blindspot monitoring. Additionally, the bike gets traction control and switchable dual-channel ABS as standard. Other features include crash alert, ride stats, find my bike, and movement alerts.Variants & PricingThe X47 is priced aggressively; the base model costs Rs 2,65,000 with the introductory offer for the first 5,000 customers; the standard pricing is Rs 3,00,000. It gets an all-new battery pack with a capacity of 7.5 kWh and 11 kWh. The 7.5 kWh pack gives a range of 227 km, and the 11 kWh pack pushes it to 343 km, both are IDC ranges.ZigSaysUltraviolette has significantly updated the core of the X47 with the new architecture. This has made the bike even more appealing because of the lighter weight and increased performance. We reckon this should make it an even more fun bike to ride. Top that off with an updated suite of features, and improved range, the X47 should be even better for everyday, as well as weekend rides. The updates should make the X47 a bike worth checking out if you are planning to buy an electric motorcycle this Diwali.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  ïpÅ!5Ç1CÉW §A95150402172026-10-22T13:26:45ZManoj Bajpayee urges action in Tiger Shroff firing case, says he hopes 'an example is set' so no one thinks of repeating such an attackThe Times of Indiatimesofindia.indiatimes.comhttps://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/hindi/bollywood/news/manoj-bajpayee-urges-action-in-tiger-shroff-firing-case-says-he-hopes-an-example-is-set-so-no-one-thinks-of-repeating-such-an-attack/articleshow/135120258.cmsengIndiaøÃúúúúúùManoj Bajpayee has appealed to the authorities to make sure that those behind the firing incident involving Tiger Shroff face consequences, saying the probe should set an example for others. Calling the incident "really scary", the actor said he was relieved that Tiger was safe and that his driver was recovering.Manoj Bajpayee on the Tiger Shroff firing incidentSpeaking to ANI, Manoj Bajpayee said the incident was worrying because shots could be fired from an unknown location, leaving people unsure about their safety."I don't see a gang war, but shots are being fired from who knows where -- that's really scary. It's sad what happened to Tiger, but the best and the happiest part is that he's safe, and his driver is also recovering slowly," Manoj Bajpayee said.The actor said he hoped the investigation would end soon and that the guilty would face the results of their actions."From what I've heard in the news, the investigation is ongoing. The alleged suspects have been brought to Mumbai, and they've been arrested too. I just hope the investigation wraps up as soon as possible and that an example is set so that next time, anyone will think twice before doing something like this," he added.Tiger Shroff firing case: 10 people arrestedMeanwhile, the police probe is moving ahead.Mumbai Police have picked up seven more people from Haryana in connection with the case, bringing the total number of arrests to 10.According to the police, two women are among the accused. The seven are expected to be brought to Mumbai for further legal steps.Earlier, the Anti-Extortion Cell of the Mumbai Police presented three accused before the Esplanade Court in Mumbai. They were brought from Kota in Rajasthan at around 3 am and produced in court after medical examinations. The court sent all three to police custody until October 31.What happened in Juhu on October 18?On October 18, three men on a motorcycle fired at Tiger Shroff's car in Mumbai's Juhu area. The actor escaped unhurt, but his driver and a passer-by were injured.Police are now looking into the alleged links between the accused and gangster Anil Pandit and how the weapon reached Mumbai. Investigators are also checking whether encrypted communication was used to plan the attack and how the suspects tried to escape after the shooting.   · ·                                                                                                                                                                                       