hursday produced Kunal (21) and Sumit (19), both hailing from Haryana's Sonipat district, and Radha (23) and Babita (23), both from Uttar Pradesh, before the Esplanade Magistrate's court here.The firing on Shroff was carried out at Pandit's instructions and he has been named as a wanted accused in the case, the police told the court.The investigators sought police custody of the four arrested accused, stating that they had actively participated in the conspiracy with those held earlier in the case.These four accused had supplied weapons to the shooters, the police said.Kunal and Summit collected a pistol and 20 rounds from Amritsar on Anil Pandit's instructions, while Radha and Babita travelled to Mumbai and handed over the weapon to other conspirators, the police said.Defence lawyers Ajinkya Mirgul and Satyavrat Joshi opposed the demand for custody, arguing that there was no material against these four accused and they had been arrested on mere suspicion.After hearing both sides, the court remanded the four accused in police custody till October 26.The October 18 Firing IncidentAt least two men fired shots at Tiger Shroff's car in Mumbai's Juhu area on the evening of October 18 before fleeing on a motorcycle. The actor escaped relatively unhurt, while his driver and a bystander were injured.The motorcycle was later found abandoned in suburban Andheri.Mumbai Police have registered a case of attempt to murder and arrested a total of 10 persons so far.On Wednesday, the court remanded Rehan alias Sukka, from Sonipat in Haryana; Ritik Kurmi alias Roshan, originally from Begusarai in Bihar and living in Mumbai; and Phool Singh from Haryana in police custody till October 31.Disclaimer: News content is sourced from the stated source. Headlines, summaries, section headers, and images are automatically generated or selected using AI/algorithms and may not always be fully accurate. Readers are advised to refer to the full article for complete context.    e trend picked up further after the West Asia war led to a rise in fuel prices. Electric vehicles now account for 8% of new car sales and 11% of new two-wheeler sales.The rapid electrification may have helped India reduce its fuel import bill, but it has accelerated import of another highly specialised commodity -- the battery cell. India's cell imports surged 94% year-on-year to '15,721 crore in the first quarter of FY27, according to commerce ministry data. In July, battery cell imports soared 159% to '7,209 crore compared with '2,772 crore in the corresponding month last year. In all of FY26, lithium-ion cell imports rose 64% to '41,667 crore. China's share is 85%. The spike in battery cell imports comes as the government is promoting electrification through incentives such as PM E Drive, PM-eBus Sewa, and the production-linked incentive (PLI) scheme for auto and auto components. The government has been trying to reduce this dependence but with limited success. To make India self-reliant in battery cells, it rolled out a Rs. 18,100-crore PLI Scheme for Advanced Chemistry Cells (ACC) in May 2021. Even though it awarded 40 GWh (gigawatt-hour) of battery cell capacity, cell production has barely begun. Except Bhavish Aggarwal-led Ola Cell Technologies, no PLI beneficiary has been able to set up cell manufacturing capacity. Ola Cell Technologies, too, has started commercial operations with only 2.5 GWh even though it bagged 20 GWh of capacity under the PLI ACC scheme. No cell maker has received any incentive yet.One reason is the dependence on imports for battery parts, mainly from China, which some say is "weaponising " access. Now, the government is planning a Rs. 12,000 crore PLI scheme for battery cell components such as cathode and anode active materials, electrolytes and copper foil separators. It wants to develop the entire raw material ecosystem.The ACC PLI Scheme has not worked as we need incentives for cell components first, Anurag Choudhary, Chairman, Managing Director and CEO of Himadri Speciality Chemical Ltd, tells BT. "Unless you have raw material components, you will remain an assembler," he says. The government must play a critical role, he says. "This is going to be a big industry," he says, adding the best option is to set up the entire value chain in the country. Himadri plans to set up a 2,000-tonne plant for cathode active materials, a key raw material for LFP (Lithium Iron Phosphate) cells, in Kolkata. The company expects it to be operational by December 2026. "We are also working on a 40,000 metric tonne plant, which will be operational in FY28."Himadri has started a 200-tonne plant in West Bengal to produce anode from coke and coal tar. "Cathode and anode account for 65% of a cell's cost," says Choudhary. Himadri plans to invest '4,200 crore for building cathode active materials; lithium carbonate comprises 25% of such materials. Since lithium is not mined in India, Himadri plans to buy from Argentina, Bolivia and Chile.Electrolyte is the only component that cell producers buy locally, while everything else comes from China, as no Indian company produces raw materials at scale, says Vikram Handa, founder and managing director of Epsilon Advanced Materials. Over 90% of cathode and anode active material is processed in China, says Handa.The government has been trying to fix things, but the industry is struggling, says Handa. "I think the intention of ACC PLI was that demand is coming and so let's promote this segment. In the last three-four years, the challenge has been that it is a high technology space that is moving extremely fast," he says.Lack of investment in technology is one reason for the situation, says Handa. "The biggest challenge is risk capital. Today, someone putting in Rs. 2,000 crore wants to earn in the next quarter. That's difficult in this                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   n  n                                                                                                  õÅ!5Å{13ÇY ∞I95150307902026-10-22T13:19:23Z'How Can Lawrence Bishnoi Claim Responsibility For Tiger Shroff Firing Incident From Jail?': Supriya Sule Seeks InquiryFree Press Journalfreepressjournal.inhttps://www.freepressjournal.in/mumbai/how-can-lawrence-bishnoi-claim-responsibility-for-tiger-shroff-firing-incident-from-jail-supriya-sule-seeks-inquiry-mumbai-newsengIndiaøœüüüüü°The attempted attack on Tiger Shroff took place on October 18, when at least two men allegedly opened fire at the actor's car in Juhu before fleeing on a motorcycle.Mumbai: Nationalist Congress Party (Sharadchandra Pawar) leader Supriya Sule on Thursday questioned how jailed gangster Lawrence Bishnoi's group could claim responsibility for the firing incident involving Bollywood actor Tiger Shroff in Mumbai's Juhu area.Reacting to reports of a social media post purportedly claiming responsibility for the attack, Sule expressed surprise that someone lodged in jail could claim involvement in such an incident.Was Surprised At News Reports: SuleSpeaking to reporters, Sule said, "I was also surprised because your news channels and newspapers say that Lawrence Bishnoi is in jail. Yet, he is tweeting himself and declaring, 'I take responsibility for this.'"She questioned how a person in jail could allegedly issue such a statement. "It is very confusing," Sule said.Call For InquiryRaising concerns over the alleged remarks by Bishnoi, Sule demanded an inquiry into the circumstances surrounding the purported claim of responsibility."Given how everyone is being intimidated, we will demand an inquiry into how someone sitting inside a jail is able to do all this," she said.Her remarks came amid an ongoing investigation into the attempted attack on Shroff.Four Accused Sent To CustodyEarlier in the day, Mumbai Police produced four accused, including two women, before a magistrate's court on Thursday in connection with the firing incident. The court remanded all four accused to police custody until October 26.The accused have been identified as Kunal (21) and Sumit (19), both residents of Sonipat district in Haryana, and Radha (23) and Babita (23), both residents of Uttar Pradesh. The four had earlier been apprehended in Delhi and Haryana.Weapons Allegedly Transported By TrainAccording to police, two of the accused allegedly travelled to Amritsar to procure weapons used in the attack.Investigators further alleged that Radha and Babita transported the firearms by train to Mumbai and handed them over directly to those involved in the firing.Firing IncidentThe firing incident took place on Sunday evening in Mumbai's Juhu area. According to the police, at least two men allegedly opened fire at Shroff's car before fleeing on a motorcycle.The actor escaped without serious injuries, while his driver and a bystander were reportedly injured. Mumbai Police said five to six rounds were fired at the vehicle during the attack.A motorcycle suspected to have been used by the shooters was subsequently recovered in an abandoned condition in Andheri.Ten Accused Arrested So FarThe Mumbai Police have registered a case under provisions relating to attempt to murder and the Arms Act. So far 10 accused have been arrested in connection with the firing incident.Investigators are continuing to examine the sequence of events, the alleged procurement and transportation of firearms, and the possible involvement of other individuals in the incident.°wÅ%5ÅQ)-Ç= æMb-95150333622026-10-22T13:21:22ZIndia has failed to build an ecosystem of batter cells. Can a new PLI change this? - BusinessTodayBusiness Todaybusinesstoday.inhttps://www.businesstoday.in/magazine/deep-dive/story/india-has-failed-to-build-an-ecosystem-of-batter-cells-can-a-new-pli-change-this-563134-2026-10-22engIndiaøµMore Indians are opting for electric cars and scooters, turning what were once niche products into an increasingly familiar sight on the roads. Th   ~   T ÅT                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        ä)Å!5w%Å{ êe95150264322026-10-22T13:16:29ZWild gaur charges at tourists on Valparai forest roadThe Hinduthehindu.comhttps://www.thehindu.com/news/national/kerala/wild-gaur-charges-at-tourists-on-valparai-forest-road/article71611634.eceengIndiaø»òòòòòóTourists heading to Chinnakallar waterfalls thrown off their bike in the attackA wild gaur charged at a group of tourists travelling towards the Chinnakallar waterfalls in Valparai on Wednesday evening, knocking two-wheeler riders off their bike. The tourists escaped without serious injuries.The incident occurred when the group was travelling along a forest road in cars and on motorcycles. The bikers failed to notice a gaur standing by the roadside and rode towards it, prompting the animal to charge at them and ram their motorcycle.The impact threw the riders and their bike onto the road. They narrowly escaped serious injury as other tourists, including those travelling in a car behind them, screamed in panic. The noise reportedly prompted the gaur to retreat into the forest, averting a potentially serious incident.The tourists said they were fortunate to have escaped with their lives.Forest officials have warned visitors to exercise caution while travelling along forest roads in the Valparai region, where wild animals are frequently sighted.í{Å%5ÅYÅ#'Åa †3b-95150303342026-10-22T13:18:52ZFour Arrested in Amritsar with 10 Kg Heroin, Foreign-Based Smuggling Links Suspected - Yes Punjab NewsYesPunjab ‚Äì No.1 News-Portal. Latest News from Punjab, India & the World.yespunjab.comhttps://yespunjab.com/four-arrested-in-amritsar-with-10-kg-heroin-foreign-based-smuggling-links-suspected/engIndiaøÕùùùùùúChandigarh/Amritsar, October 22, 2026 (Yes Punjab News)Punjab Police have arrested four alleged members of a narcotics smuggling network and recovered 10 kg of heroin from their possession in Amritsar, Director General of Police (DGP) Gaurav Yadav said on Thursday.The arrests were made by the Counter Intelligence (CI) unit in Amritsar as part of the state government's ongoing drive to make Punjab drug-free, being pursued under the directions of Chief Minister Bhagwant Singh Mann.The arrested accused have been identified as Sukhraj Singh, a resident of Hargobind Avenue; Ajitpal Singh alias Mota, a resident of Khandwala; Anmolpreet Singh, a resident of Jawahar Nagar; and Robin Gill, a resident of Chogawan village. All four belong to Amritsar district.Police teams also impounded a Hero Splendor motorcycle and a black Honda Activa scooter allegedly used to transport the heroin consignment.According to DGP Gaurav Yadav, preliminary investigations suggest that the accused were allegedly working in coordination with foreign-based smugglers and receiving heroin consignments for further distribution in Punjab.Sharing details of the operation, the DGP said the CI Amritsar team had received reliable intelligence that four associates of a foreign-based handler had retrieved a large consignment of heroin and were expected to deliver it to another party.Acting on the input, police teams intercepted the four suspects near Dera Baba Darshan Singh in Ghanupur Kale village while they were travelling on a motorcycle and a scooter. A search led to the recovery of 10 kg of heroin, police said.Yadav said further investigation was underway to establish the complete forward and backward linkages of the network, identify other individuals involved and dismantle the wider cross-border narcotics smuggling operation.A case has been registered under FIR No. 67, dated October 22, 2026, under Sections 21, 25 and 29 of the Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances (NDPS) Act at the Police Station, State Special Operations Cell (SSOC), Amritsar.     (NSE: CREDITACC, BSE: 541770, 'CA Grameen'), India's leading rural-focused inclusive financing platform, today announced its unaudited and limited reviewed financial performance for the second quarter and first half of the financial year 2026-27.Business Highlights: Q2 FY27* AUM grew 19.5% YoY from 25,903 crore to INR 30,946 crore* Disbursements increased by 19.4% YoY from INR 5,322 crore to INR 6,355 crore* Retail Finance portfolio share at 23.3% in Sep-26 Vs 20.6% in Jun-26* Healthy new borrower addition of 2.3 lakh with 35% being New-to-Credit (NTC) & 4.9 lakh in H1 FY27* PAR 0+ stood at 2.1% in Q2 FY27 with PAR 90+ at 1.1%* X-Bucket Collection Efficiency stood at 99.71% in Sep-26* Branch network grew by 5.3% YoY from 2,209 in Sep-25 to 2,326 branches in Sep-26* Employee base grew by 4.5% YoY from 21,701 to 22,677Financial Highlights: Q2 FY27* Total income increased by 26.2% YoY to INR 1,904.8 crore* Pre-provision operating profit (PPOP) increased 39.6 % YoY to INR 970.2 crore* Credit cost declined by 43.2% YoY to INR 299 crore* Profit Before Tax (PBT) grew 296.6% YoY from INR 169.2 crore to INR 670.9 crore* Profit After Tax (PAT) grew 298.7% YoY from INR 125.8 crore to INR 501.6 crore* GNPA / NNPA measured predominantly at 60+ dpd stood 1.79% / 0.59%, with PAR 90+ of 1.11%* Robust liquidity of INR 3,501.9 crore of cash, cash equivalents, and investments, 10.2% of the total assets* Robust capital position with a CRAR of 26.9%* Credit Rating: AA-/Stable by CRISIL, ICRA & India RatingCommenting on the business performance, MrGanesh Narayanan, Managing Director and Chief Executive Officer of CreditAccess Grameen Ltd, said, "We reported our highest quarterly PAT of INR 502 crore in Q2 FY27, having grown five-fold over the past five yearsFY27 marks a clear turnaround in profitability, recording INR 995 crore PAT in H1 FY27 as against INR 778 crore PAT in FY26Our H1 FY27 ROA stood at 5.9% and ROE at 23.8%, reflecting strong business performanceOur ROA guidance has been revised upward from 4.0-4.8% (earlier) to 5.2-5.8% (revised) and ROE guidance from 16.0-20.0% (earlier) to 21.0-24.0% (revised) while retaining our AUM growth at 20.0-25.0% and credit cost at 3.0-4.0%We have reduced our loan pricing by 50 bps applicable from October month in line with our board approved pricing policy.CA Grameen stands today as a more diversified and profitable company than at any point in its historyOur transformation into a diversified, rural-focused lender is now visible at scale with Retail Finance share at 23% of the AUMWe are increasingly working towards serving the entire customer household's financial needs, from business loans to financing mortgages, affordable housing and two wheelersFor H2 FY27, our focus is to grow responsibly while staying vigilant amidst evolving macroeconomic environmentWe will remain cautious in districts showing drought-related challenges, while meeting the growing credit needs of our seasoned customers, and stepping up new customer acquisitionAs we enter the seasonally stronger second half, we do so with momentum in our business, strength in our balance sheet and confidence in delivering on our aforesaid guidance."About CreditAccess Grameen LimitedCreditAccess Grameen Limited is India's leading rural-focused inclusive financing platform headquartered in BengaluruThe Company provides a curated lifecycle credit suite to low-middle income households spanning group loans, individual business loans, secured business loans, affordable housing loans, and two-wheeler financingThe Company operates across 460 districts in 16 states (Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Chhattisgarh, Goa, Gujarat, Jharkhand, Karnataka, Kerala, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Odisha, Rajasthan,                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      