receive treatment at Kelhari.An injured pregnant woman was referred to Ambikapur Medical College after her unborn baby was reported to have died.Children were among those injured in the crash.Preliminary accounts suggested a possible steering failure, but the cause of the crash was yet to be established.One killed under Bastar Dussehra Vijay RathThe MCB tragedy came a day after a fatal accident during the centuries-old Bastar Dussehra celebrations in Jagdalpur, where one man was killed and two others seriously injured after coming under the massive eight-wheeled Vijay Rath during the Bhitar Raini ritual late Wednesday.The deceased was identified as Koparam Kashyap of Surguda village in Bastanar block. One of the injured was identified as Sukhram Mandavi of Lohandiguda, while the identity of the other injured person was yet to be established.Koparam was caught under the chariot while it was being pulled as part of the traditional ritual and died at the spot. Another man suffered a grievous leg injury after coming under a wheel and was rushed to Maharani Hospital. A third person was injured at Lalbagh after reportedly coming in contact with the moving chariot.The accident assumes added significance as President Droupadi Murmu is scheduled to be in Jagdalpur on Friday to attend the Muria Darbar and other programmes associated with Bastar Dussehra. Thousands of tribal villagers, devotees and visitors gather for the festival, one of Bastar's most important cultural and religious traditions.The Vijay Rath is pulled by tribal villagers as part of rituals associated with Maa Danteshwari. The sheer size of the wooden chariot and the large crowds surrounding it make crowd regulation and maintaining a safe distance around its wheels critical during its movement.Two killed in separate Raigarh crashesTwo more people died and two were injured in separate road accidents in Raigarh district.In Kharsia, Piyush Chauhan, 24, was returning after visiting a Dussehra fair with friends Devendra Sahis and Akash Mahant when their scooter collided with an oncoming motorcycle on the Chhote Mudpar main road.Piyush suffered severe head injuries and died at the spot, while Devendra and Akash were injured and taken to hospital. Police have registered a case against the motorcycle rider and are investigating the circumstances of the collision.In another accident under Chhal police station, a man identified as Shobha Dhanwar of Kharra died after his motorcycle went off the road and struck a tree. Villagers spotted him on Thursday morning and informed police.   ñ ñ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            îfÅ%5Å#ÇE §	b-95150131542026-10-22T13:06:05ZNot possible for jailed person to plan attack and claim responsibility on social media: Supriya Sule on Tiger Shroff caseIANS Liveianslive.inhttps://ianslive.in/not-possible-for-jailed-person-to-plan-attack-and-claim-responsibility-on-social-media-supriya-sule-on-tiger-shroff-case--20261022165623engIndiaø“RRRRRSMumbai, Oct 22 (IANS) Responding to the firing incident targeting Bollywood actor Tiger Shroff, for which jailed gangster Lawrence Bishnoi has claimed responsibility, Nationalist Congress Party (SP) MP Supriya Sule said on Thursday that no jailed person can plan an attack and then take responsibility and post it on social media.Sule said, "I was surprised because your news channels and newspapers say that Lawrence Bishnoi is in jail. Yet, he is tweeting himself and declaring, 'I take responsibility for this.' It is hard to comprehend how a person inside a jail can take responsibility like this. It is very confusing. Given how everyone is being intimidated, we will demand an inquiry into how someone sitting inside a jail can do all this."On Thursday, Mumbai Police Crime Branch brought in four more accused, two women, Radha and Babita, and Kunal and Sumit, to Killa Court on transit remand in connection with the case.Meanwhile, police sources revealed that the accused practised shooting at a location near Mumbai before the attack. A misfire also reportedly occurred while they were testing the weapon in their hotel room in Andheri.Reportedly, the accused actually had another Bollywood celebrity in mind. However, as per the reports, when the accused were unable to easily reach their initially planned target, they decided to target Tiger Shroff instead.The accused reportedly learned through social media reels shared by Tiger's fans that he did not have police protection at that time, and keeping this in mind, they decided to target him.During the preliminary questioning, it has also been revealed that the attack on Tiger was intended to spread fear in the industry.On 18 October evening, Tiger had a narrow escaped after three motorcycle-borne men opened fire at him in the Juhu area of Mumbai. While the actor did not suffer any injury in the attack, his driver was injured in the incident.Within hours of the incident, a video clip was circulating on social media in which a man claiming to be Anil Pandit from the Lawrence Bishnoi gang took responsibility for the firing and claimed that it was a warning to the actor to stop working with superstar Salman Khan.The three accused were arrested at Kota railway station in Rajasthan within hours of the incident.   ñ áñ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          °dÅ!5ÅYÇ+ æM95150106072026-10-22T13:04:26ZTiger Shroff firing: Attack allegedly ordered by Lawrence Bishnoi gang member Anil Pandit; 5 more helddtNext.indtnext.inhttps://www.dtnext.in/entertainment/cinema/tiger-shroff-firing-attack-allegedly-ordered-by-lawrence-bishnoi-gang-member-anil-pandit-5-more-heldengIndiaøﬂ_____`MUMBAI: Actor Tiger Shroff was fired upon allegedly at the behest of Lawrence Bishnoi gang member Anil Pandit, the Mumbai Police told a court on Wednesday, even as t   ÜîuÅ!5Å}%-Çk £w95150125282026-10-22T13:05:31ZTiger Shroff firing case: Two women allegedly smuggled guns by train to Mumbai, sent to police custody- Moneycontrol.comMoneyControlmoneycontrol.comhttps://www.moneycontrol.com/entertainment/bollywood/tiger-shroff-firing-case-two-women-allegedly-smuggled-guns-by-train-to-mumbai-sent-to-police-custody-article-14057887.htmlengIndiaø·qqqqqrA court here on Thursday remanded four accused, including two women, held in Delhi in connection with the firing at actor Tiger Shroff to police custody till October 26. They have been identified as Kunal (21) and Sumit (19), both residents of Haryana's Sonipat district, and Radha (23) and Babita (23), both from Uttar Pradesh. The accused were produced before the magistrate court after being brought to the city from the national capital. Also Read: Tiger Shroff's team shares video of his injured arms after firing attack amid trolling, writes 'God is kind' Police sought their remand, saying they have active participation in the conspiracy along with the previously arrested accused. Police said these accused supplied weapons to the shooters, which were used in the crime. The defence, represented by Ajinkya Mirgul and Satyavrat Joshi, opposed their remand, saying that there was no material against them and they have been arrested on the basis of mere suspicion. Hence, their custodial interrogation is unwarranted and the accused should be remanded to judicial custody, they said. After hearing both sides, the court remanded the four accused to custody till October 26 instead of October 31. According to the police, two of these accused had travelled to Amritsar to procure the weapons, while the two women smuggled the firearms by train to Mumbai, handing them over directly to the attackers. At least two men fired gunshots at Shroff's car in Mumbai's Juhu area on Sunday evening before speeding away on a motorcycle. The actor escaped relatively unhurt, while his driver and a bystander were injured. According to Mumbai Police, five to six rounds were fired at the car. A motorcycle, allegedly used by the shooters, was found abandoned in Andheri. Also Read: Tiger Shroff firing case: New CCTV shows accused leaving Andheri hotel with blue bag allegedly carrying gun The Mumbai Police have registered a case under the provisions of attempt to murder and the Arms Act. A total of 10 persons have been arrested in the case so far. Of them, the court on Wednesday remanded three accused- Rehan alias Sukka, from Sonipat in Haryana; Ritik Kurmi alias Roshan, originally from Begusarai in Bihar and living in Mumbai; and Phool Singh from Haryana- to police custody till October 31.    he probe expanded to unravel a firearm-supplying network and other aspects following the arrest of five more persons..Out of the eight individuals held so far, three key accused allegedly involved in firing at Shroff's car were brought to Mumbai from Kota in Rajasthan on Wednesday morning and sent to police custody till October 31.Investigators told the court that the attack was allegedly carried out on the directions of Lawrence Bishnoi gang member Anil Pandit and further probe was required.The crime branch of the Mumbai Police, which is probing the case, produced Rehan alias Sukka, from Sonipat in Haryana; Ritik Kurmi alias Roshan, originally from Begusarai in Bihar and living in Mumbai; and Phool Singh from Haryana, before a magistrate court and sought their custody for 14 days for a detailed investigation.The police told the court that they needed to recover the weapons used in the crime and that the accused had destroyed two mobile phones.The custody was required to unearth the conspiracy and the motive behind the incident, the police said, adding they needed to collect the electronic evidence, including the call data records..The police told the court that the accused were arrested based on the analysis of CCTV footage and tracking of their bike.Defence lawyers Ajinkya Mirgal and Satyabrat Joshi, however, opposed the police remand, saying the custody was not required for the recovery of weapons.They also submitted that the accused have been interrogated during their transit remand.The court, after hearing both sides, remanded the accused to police custody till October 31.Tiger Shroff escaped relatively unhurt after two assailants fired gunshots at his car in the Juhu area on Sunday evening. His driver and a bystander were injured.A motorcycle, allegedly used by the shooters, was found abandoned in Andheri.The three accused were nabbed from a train in Kota the following day.Meanwhile, Deputy Commissioner of Police Raj Tilak Roshan said five individuals, including two women, have been apprehended from Haryana and Uttar Pradesh, and are being brought to Mumbai..He said sustained technical analysis and interrogation led the crime branch to the wider syndicate responsible for procuring and supplying the firearms used in the shooting.Acting on precise intelligence and with the coordinated assistance of the Delhi and Haryana police, a series of simultaneous raids were conducted, the DCP said while addressing a news conference.Delhi Police had identified four arrested accused as: Kunal (21) and Sumit (19), both residents of Haryana's Sonipat district, and Radha (23) and Babita (23), both from Uttar Pradesh.Roshan said a deeply coordinated conspiracy is being unravelled during the investigation."Two of the arrested suspects had travelled to Amritsar to procure the weapons. The two female suspects then smuggled the firearms by train to Mumbai, handing them over directly to the attackers."The newly-arrested male suspect had stayed back in Mumbai alongside the shooters to provide logistical support and local assistance," Roshan added.."Two of the arrested suspects had travelled to Amritsar to procure the weapons. The two female suspects then smuggled the firearms by train to Mumbai, handing them over directly to the attackers."The newly-arrested male suspect had stayed back in Mumbai alongside the shooters to provide logistical support and local assistance," Roshan added.According to Delhi Police, Kunal allegedly collected a pistol and 20 rounds from Amritsar on the instructions of Anil Pandit, a 32-year-old US-based gangster from Panipat and an alleged associate of the Lawrence Bishnoi gang, with Sumit accompanying him. Radha and Babita allegedly travelled to Mumbai with the weapon.The Anti-Extortion Cell (AEC) of th                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    ° í°                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     