hari met with an accident, killing five people. More than 30 passengers were on board. The accident occurred near Pahad Hanswahi. The injured were taken to Kelhari Hospital.Five deaths have been reported so far. Fifteen people are seriously injured. The bus owner has also died. The incident occurred under the jurisdiction of Kelhari police station.In August, a 24-year-old Mumbai woman was killed after a trailer truck struck her motorcycle on National Highway 30 in Chhattisgarh's Dhamtari district.Riddhi Thakkar, who lived in Kandivali West, was part of a group of about 23 motorcyclists travelling from Mumbai to explore Bastar. Police have begun investigating the crash and are trying to trace the vehicle and identify its driver, who allegedly fled the scene.Police said most members of the group had ridden ahead, while Thakkar and a few others were following behind. Between 5 am and 6 am, the trailer hit her motorcycle from the rear near Markatola Ghat on NH-30.The collision flung Thakkar onto the road, leaving her critically injured. Emergency responders took her to a nearby health centre after being alerted to the accident.Doctors administered initial treatment before referring her to Shriram Hospital in Dhamtari because of the seriousness of her injuries. She was declared dead after being taken to the hospital.The trailer driver allegedly escaped with the vehicle following the collision. Police subsequently arrived at the site, opened an investigation and began efforts to locate the trailer and establish the driver's identity.   – –                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      ñ,ÅU!5ÅÇ1 ®+95148738142026-10-22T11:23:28ZTiger Shroff Shooters' Secret Hideout: The Mystery Of Room No. 201NDTVndtv.comhttps://www.ndtv.com/india-news/tiger-shroff-lawrence-bishnoi-salman-khan-tiger-shroff-shooters-secret-hideout-the-mystery-of-room-no-201-12220136engIndiaø¿êêêêêèThe shooting took place in Mumbai's Juhu area on Sunday evening.Mumbai:For five days, Room No. 201 in a modest hotel in Mumbai's Andheri East locality played host to two men who opened fire at Bollywood actor Tiger Shroff. The room cost approximately Rs 1,500 a day, and the men staying there had identified themselves as residents of Haryana's Hisar, concealing their identities while preparing for the attack.Phool Singh and Rehan Singh, the two shooters, checked into the hotel on the morning of October 14, arriving between 11 am and 11:30 am. They remained there until the afternoon of October 18, when they checked out between 4 pm and 4:30 pm, shortly before the firing incident in Mumbai's Juhu area. They never returned to the hotel.According to sources, the men would leave the hotel each day in an auto-rickshaw to conduct reconnaissance, examining locations and making preparations for the attack. The police are also examining an incident in which a pistol supplied to the group was being tested at the hideout when a round misfired, although the men allegedly managed to conceal what had happened.The hotel staff learnt about the episode only when the police arrived on October 20, two days after the alleged shooters had checked out.Three men accused of carrying out the attack were arrested in Rajasthan's Kota earlier this week as they allegedly planned to flee to Nepal. The Kota city police arrested them from a train on Monday before handing them over to the Mumbai Police.The accused were brought to Mumbai early on Wednesday and formally arrested before being produced in a magistrate's court. The three have been identified as Rehan alias Sukka, 18, from Sonipat in Haryana; Ritik Kurmi alias Roshan, 22, originally from Begusarai in Bihar and living in Mumbai; and Phool Singh, 18, from Haryana.The shooting took place in Mumbai's Juhu area on Sunday evening, when at least two men opened fire at Shroff's car before escaping on a motorcycle.The actor escaped relatively unhurt, but his driver and a bystander sustained injuries. According to the Mumbai Police, five to six rounds were fired at the vehicle.A motorcycle believed to have been used by the attackers was later found abandoned in Andheri.The police have registered a case under provisions relating to attempt to murder and the Arms Act.Got a follow‚Äëup question on this article? Go onHow may i help you todayShow full articleTrack Latest News Live on NDTV.com and get news updates from India and around the worldTiger Shroff, Lawrence Bishnoi, Salman Khan   ù ù                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   ö_ÅV%5ÅÅS1Åu Øub-95148724562026-10-22T11:22:28ZAston Martin AMB 001 Is a Turbocharged Superbike With Hypercar DNABeautiful Life | web design, industrial design, art works, interior design, graphic design and morebeautifullife.infohttps://www.beautifullife.info/automotive-design/aston-martin-amb-001-is-a-turbocharged-superbike-with-hypercar-dna/engIndia?…ôôôôôöAston Martin is best known for its elegant grand tourers and high-performance supercars, but the AMB 001 takes the British marque into an entirely different territory. Developed in collaboration with historic motorcycle manufacturer Brough Superior, this limited-edition superbike brings Aston Martin's design language to two wheels, combining sculptural carbon-fiber bodywork with an unusual turbocharged engine and an exclusive, track-focused character.First unveiled in 2019, the AMB 001 was conceived as a motorcycle that would look just as striking standing still as it would moving at speed. Its flowing bodywork, sharp aerodynamic surfaces, and signature Stirling Green finish with lime accents give it an unmistakable Aston Martin identity. Carbon fiber, machined aluminum, and titanium components contribute to its lightweight construction, while the low, aggressive profile makes the bike look closer to a miniature hypercar than a conventional superbike.Power comes from a 997cc, 88-degree V-twin engine equipped with a turbocharger. The original AMB 001 produces around 180 horsepower, an extraordinary output for a motorcycle of this size. A six-speed gearbox sends power to the rear wheel, while the lightweight construction helps keep the dry weight around 180 kilograms. Although it was never designed to compete directly with the fastest bikes in the world in terms of outright top speed, its turbocharged powertrain and exceptional power-to-weight ratio make it a compelling example of high-performance motorcycle engineering.The chassis is equally distinctive. Rather than relying on a conventional telescopic front fork, the AMB 001 uses a Fior-style front suspension system with articulated arms. This unusual arrangement separates steering and suspension functions in a different way from traditional forks, contributing to the motorcycle's distinctive engineering character. Carbon-fiber bodywork and carefully sculpted aerodynamic elements complete the design.The AMB 001 is not road legal and was developed exclusively for track use. Production was limited to just 100 examples, each hand-built by Brough Superior in Toulouse, France. Its rarity, unusual turbocharged powertrain, and Aston Martin styling have made it a particularly distinctive proposition for collectors who appreciate motorcycles as both engineering objects and design pieces.The collaboration later produced the more extreme AMB 001 Pro, which increased output to 225 horsepower and drew inspiration from Aston Martin's Valkyrie AMR Pro hypercar. Limited to 88 units, the Pro version pushed the original concept further with revised aerodynamics and a more track-oriented setup.For Aston Martin, the AMB 001 represents an experiment beyond four wheels. For Brough Superior, it offers an opportunity to combine specialist motorcycle engineering with one of the world's most recognizable automotive design identities. The result is a rare machine that brings together two different traditions of performance culture in a thoroughly unconventional package.   Ω ÆΩ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 °7ÅX!5Å#'Ç æM95148687632026-10-22T11:19:11ZUltraviolette X-47 Venom launched at Rs 2.65 lakh, gets 343km rangeIndia Todayindiatoday.inhttps://www.indiatoday.in/auto/bikes/story/ultraviolette-x-47-venom-launched-at-rs-265-lakh-gets-343km-range-3022776-2026-10-22engIndia?¿êêêêêèUltraviolette has launched the X-47 Venom at Rs 2.65 lakh, featuring a 343km claimed range, 155kmph top speed, 12kW DC fast charging and a new 100V architecture.Ultraviolette has launched the X-47 Venom, the    ªíNÅW!5ÅI!Ç †M95148704312026-10-22T11:20:34ZBengaluru: Bihar man arrested for killing wife, dumping body in drainAsian News International (ANI)aninews.inhttps://www.aninews.in/news/national/general-news/bengaluru-bihar-man-arrested-for-killing-wife-dumping-body-in-drain20261022164738/engIndiaø”SSSSSTBengaluru (Karnataka) [India], October 22 (ANI): The Bengaluru police have arrested a 22-year-old migrant labourer from Bihar for allegedly murdering his 25-year-old wife and dumping her body in a stormwater drain over suspicions of infidelity, officials said on Thursday.The accused has been identified as Mohammad Irfaj, while the victim has been identified as his wife, Ruksana. Both are natives of Bihar.According to police, the couple had married approximately a year ago. Ruksana was previously married and had a child from her first marriage. The duo reportedly fell in love while selling balloons in Bihar and relocated to Bengaluru around six months ago, where Irfaj secured employment at a local garment factory.Police said the marital relationship had turned strained in recent months, as Irfaj suspected Ruksana of having an affair, alleging she was frequently talking to someone on the phone. The couple frequently quarrelled over the same.The dispute reached a violent flashpoint on October 10, when Irfaj allegedly attacked Ruksana with a stone, killing her on the spot, before stuffing a piece of cloth into her mouth, investigators said.To conceal the crime, the accused allegedly broke the handle lock of an unattended motorcycle parked nearby. He then stuffed the victim's body into a bag, placed it between the handlebars, pushed the vehicle to the Gopalapura drainage area, and dumped the body into the stormwater drain.On October 13, the body -- wrapped in a plastic cover -- was discovered in the drain. Initially, police were unable to identify the victim.The Magadi Road police then constituted more than 10 teams to conduct extensive door-to-door verifications and cross-reference recent missing person complaints in the locality.During the inquiry, local residents alerted the police that a woman residing in Gopalapura had been missing for several days, leading to Irfaj's questioning.As per the police, during the interrogation, the accused allegedly confessed.Further investigation is underway, and more details are awaited. (ANI)    next-generation version of its electric crossover motorcycle, alongside its new VX-100 100V architecture. The motorcycle gets a claimed IDC range of 343km, a top speed of 155kmph and support for 12kW DC fast charging. Prices start at Rs 2.65 lakh (ex-showroom), with deliveries scheduled to begin in November 2026.The introductory price is applicable to the first 5,000 customers. Bookings open on October 22 at 4pm through the company's website and its UV Space Stations across India. Ultraviolette will also launch the X-47 Venom in Europe at EICMA 2026 in Milan on November 3.Ultraviolette X-47 Venom: Performance and rangeThe X-47 Venom produces 40bhp and 550Nm of torque at the wheel. Ultraviolette claims a 0-60kmph acceleration time of 2.7 seconds and a 0-100kmph time of 5.9 seconds. The motorcycle has a top speed of 155kmph and a kerb weight of 180kg, making it up to 20kg lighter than the previous-generation platform.Power comes from the new UV LIGHTFURY electric motor, which uses soft magnetic composite (SMC) technology. The motor has been developed to reduce weight and energy losses while improving power density. Ultraviolette has also introduced a new motor control unit that is twice as compact and offers three times the power density, according to the company.The motorcycle gets a 1.6kW onboard charger for conventional charging, alongside support for 12kW external DC fast charging. Ultraviolette claims the X-47 Venom is the only electric two-wheeler in India capable of charging at this power level.New VX-100 platform and battery technologyThe X-47 Venom is the first motorcycle built on Ultraviolette's new VX-100 100V architecture. The platform combines the SRB-11 battery technology, a new motor and a revised motor control unit, with the aim of improving performance, efficiency and thermal management while reducing weight.The SRB-11 battery features a 100V configuration and offers up to 20% higher energy density and twice the cooling performance, as claimed by the company. An optimised thermal management system is designed to dissipate heat up to 50% faster. The battery is housed in a cast-aluminium enclosure and has an IP67 rating for protection against dust and water immersion.Safety equipment includes a battery management system with a pyro-fuse designed to isolate the electrical circuit within microseconds when a critical fault is detected. The battery pack also gets cell-level fuses. Ultraviolette says the VX-100 architecture is modular and can be adapted for different battery capacities, vehicle configurations and power outputs in future products.Radar-based safety features and new technologyThe X-47 Venom retains Ultraviolette's UV Hypersense radar system, which uses a 77GHz rear radar to monitor traffic up to 200 metres away. The system supports blind-spot monitoring, lane-change assistance, overtake alerts and rear-collision warnings. In the event of a potential rear collision, the motorcycle can activate its hazard lights and alert the rider through the integrated mirrors.Other features include a new 7-inch TFT LCD touchscreen called UV INTERFACE X, cruise control, a rear-camera feed with Park Assist and Mappls-based navigation. The motorcycle also gets VIOLETTE A.I. connected functions, including movement, fall and towing alerts, remote lockdown, crash alerts, Delta Watch and an anti-collision warning system.Ultraviolette has also introduced B-MAX, a reusable safety vest designed to extend rider protection beyond the motorcycle. The company claims the vest meets twice the CE safety standards and offers 86% force attenuation. It covers areas including the collar, back, chest, sides and hips, and incorporates a cervical brace, spinal protection and thorax chambers. The vest can be reus                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  ˜ 	• ¥Ë ˜                                 