nom electric bike with 343-km range, claims 10x lower running costs- Moneycontrol.comMoneyControlmoneycontrol.comhttps://www.moneycontrol.com/automobile/ultraviolette-unveils-x47-venom-electric-bike-with-343-km-range-claims-10x-lower-running-costs-article-14057788.htmlengIndia?Ω"Bengaluru-based electric motorcycle maker Ultraviolette has unveiled the X47 Venom, its latest performance electric bike with a cla   ΩåWÅY!5k1CÅ} ï95148670032026-10-22T11:17:50ZMan drowns during Durga idol immersion in ManerThe Times of Indiatimesofindia.indiatimes.comhttps://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/patna/man-drowns-during-durga-idol-immersion-in-maner/articleshow/135117317.cmsengIndiaøÿÿÿÿÿÿÿPatna: A 25-year-old man drowned during the immersion of Goddess Durga's idol at Ramghat artificial pond at Maner in Patna district late Wednesday night after his foot got trapped in the wooden structure beneath the idol, police said.The deceased, Shashi Kumar, son of Suresh Paswan, was a resident of Newati Mohalla. According to local residents, Shashi was participating in the immersion procession when he entered deep water along with the idol. His foot got stuck in the wooden structure, causing him to lose his balance and drown.Locals pulled him out of the water and rushed him to Maner Primary Health Centre (PHC), where doctors declared him dead. Hoping to save him, his family then took him to a private doctor before rushing him to ESIC Hospital in Bihta. However, doctors there also declared him dead.The family alleged that ESIC Hospital management did not provide an ambulance to transport the body, forcing them to return to Maner with it on a motorcycle.The family also accused doctors at the Maner PHC of negligence, claiming that Shashi was breathing when he was pulled out of the water but was declared dead without receiving proper treatment.Maner police station SHO Rajneesh Kumar said that, based on the report received from PHC doctors that Shashi died by drowning, police are conducting further legal proceedings in the case.    imed range of 343 kilometres on a single charge and running costs nearly 10 times lower than petrol-powered motorcycles. Built on the company's newly developed VX100 platform, the X47 Venom can accelerate from 0 to 100 kmph in 5.9 seconds and deliver 550 Nm of torque at the wheel. The company said the platform was developed over two years and would serve as the core architecture for its future motorcycle portfolio. Ultraviolette said the new motorcycle is designed to address the trade-offs between power, weight, acceleration and efficiency that have traditionally shaped the performance motorcycle segment. Explaining the cost advantage, the company said Rs 1,000 spent on petrol would purchase around 10 litres of fuel, allowing a performance motorcycle above 300cc, delivering mileage of 30 kilometres per litre, to travel approximately 300 kilometres. In comparison, the same amount spent on electricity would purchase around 100 units, which the company claimed could power the X47 Venom over a substantially longer distance. Ultraviolette said its calculations indicated a roughly tenfold reduction in running costs. The comparison was based on the company's assumptions about petrol prices, power tariffs and motorcycle efficiency. The X47 Venom's claimed 343-km range is based on the Indian Driving Cycle (IDC). Also read: Ultraviolette bags $85 million Series E led by Yali, TDK Ventures; Lip-Bu Tan invests New platform promises lighter, faster motorcycles At the centre of the X47 Venom is Ultraviolette's VX100 platform, which incorporates a redesigned battery pack, electric motor and motor controller to improve performance while reducing weight. The motorcycle uses an 11 kWh battery pack, called SRB 11, which the company claims is 15 percent lighter than the previous generation despite offering higher capacity. Ultraviolette said it has improved battery energy density by 25 percent and doubled cooling performance through changes to its thermal management system. The company has also developed a new electric motor, UV Light Fury, which uses aerospace-derived materials and engineering techniques to reduce weight by around 25-30 percent while maintaining sustained power output across the RPM range. Its redesigned motor controller is 40 percent lighter than the previous generation and capable of delivering 30 kW of power. The controller uses silicon carbide power transistors and can deliver more than 1,000 amps of current, it said. According to Ultraviolette, the VX100 platform draws on engineering developments from its F99 racing programme, including learnings from its 400-volt architecture. The company said the platform was developed in two years, compared with the five to seven years typically required to develop an entirely new motorcycle platform or powertrain. Focus on regenerative braking and safety The X47 Venom also comes with nine levels of regenerative braking, allowing riders to adjust energy recovery depending on riding conditions. Ultraviolette said regenerative braking is available from 99 percent state of charge downwards, with the improved thermal management system allowing the battery to recover energy more efficiently. The company said the system can recover more than 10 kW of power through regenerative braking. On safety, Ultraviolette has introduced a pyrofuse mechanism designed to isolate the battery's electrical circuit within two to three milliseconds of detecting an abnormality. The company claimed it is the first two-wheeler manufacturer globally to introduce the technology. The mechanism adds another layer to the company's existing battery safety architecture, which includes thermal, electrical, mechanical, electronic and software-based protections. Ultraviolette said riders of its existing F77 and X47 motorcycles have collectively covered more than 40 million kilometres across India                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   027 could be a difficult year for startups and said "it won't be a bad idea" for entrepreneurs to raise funds now as access to capital could become difficult in the months ahead.Speaking at the NDTV World Summit 2026, Kamath said the AI industry was attracting enormous investments but had yet to generate enough revenue to justify the spending."The amount of money going into AI and the money coming out of AI, there is a big gap in between that," he said.Kamath estimated that around $1 trillion was being invested in AI annually, while revenue generated through enterprise adoption was between $100 billion and $200 billion. This leaves a gap of $700 billion to $800 billion a year that investors or other sources of funding must cover, he said.He noted that foreign capital, including investments from the Gulf, helped fund American AI companies in the previous funding cycle. Finding enough fresh capital to sustain investments at the current scale every year could prove difficult.Kamath said investors were counting on AI to boost productivity and generate trillions of dollars in economic benefits to justify the huge spending. "I don't know if I've seen that happen on the ground yet," he said.He also warned that rising US Treasury yields could make borrowing more expensive. "So for next year, with American Treasury rates going up, cost of money going to 5-6-7%, I don't know where it'll go, it's at 5% today.""Somebody has to bridge this gap up until that five trillion dollar revenue story plays out, even if it does or it doesn't, nobody knows," he said.Kamath advised founders to secure funding while investor appetite remained relatively strong. "So if somebody is able to raise a lot of capital today and keep it, probably not a bad idea," he said.Kamath is the co-founder of Zerodha, one of India's largest stockbroking firms. He has also invested in several businesses, including Licious, Nourish You, Pee Safe, Subko and electric scooter maker Ather.He is involved in philanthropy and environmental initiatives and has signed the Giving Pledge, a commitment to donate at least half of one's wealth to charitable causes during one's lifetime or through a will.At the summit, Kamath also backed the idea of an inheritance tax, calling it "probably not the worst idea in the world".An inheritance tax is levied on beneficiaries who receive money, property or other assets after someone's death. Kamath questioned whether wealth passed between generations should be treated differently from income earned during a person's lifetime."I don't like the idea of taxing somebody who is earning money in his lifetime and incentivising that but taxing people between generations when wealth is being passed through; I don't know in what manner. It has to be done in a manner where it's not an unrealized gain," he said.He said that such a tax should not force business owners to sell equity in their companies to meet their tax obligations. He also recalled facing online criticism when he had previously shared his views on inheritance tax.Kamath also discussed the Gini coefficient, a measure of income or wealth inequality. He said inequality was worsening in many parts of the world and argued that the wealth gap would be difficult to reduce unless wages grew alongside asset prices."As long as wage growth in society is not going to keep up with asset price inflation, logically it's impossible for wealth inequality to come down," he said, adding that governments might need to intervene to address the problem.He also asked wealthy individuals to take steps to reduce inequality before it becomes more difficult to tackle."To the rich people in this room, it's not even altruism, but it could be self-serving to solve this problem when you can, before you reach a point where you have t                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                India's used electric two-wheeler market over the next three years as it examines resale values, battery health and long-term ownership patterns. Once the company is sufficiently satisfied with the data it has collated, will it adopt a different - perhaps, more aggressive - approach to the EV segment unlike now when it is treading relatively more cautiously.* Company wants to address residual value concerns among EVs* HMSI studying the market before adopting a more aggressive approach* Battery health one of the key parameters Honda is focusing onCompany says second-hand EV market could potentially support continued EV growthCEO believes that, like today's smartphones, EVs will also need a reliable indicator of overall healthHonda is studying how customers respond to electric two-wheelers after several years of use, particularly as the first generation of vehicles enters the pre-owned market. The assessment includes battery replacement requirements and the value of electric vehicles over their operating lives.In an exclusive interaction with our sister concern, Autocar Professional, HMSI President and CEO Tsutsumu Otani said, "We are analysing how to address residual value and how to maintain vehicle value over time. This is a key issue, not just launching new models."Asked about the timeline for assessing the used EV market before Honda considers a more aggressive approach towards electric two-wheelers in India, Otani said, "Perhaps within the next three years we will have a clearer understanding."He noted that the first wave of electric vehicles entered the market around five years ago, in 2021, and that Honda was beginning to observe how customers responded to used EVs. The company is also examining how vehicle values can be preserved as these products age.Otani said motorcycle customers have historically expected to use their vehicles for more than ten years, while electric vehicles bring additional considerations related to battery replacement and residual value."We would like to study how the second-hand EV market develops because it could support continued EV growth," he said. Honda is examining customer reactions after purchasing EVs, including how the second-hand market develops as more vehicles complete their initial ownership periods.The company is also conducting studies on battery health. Otani confirmed that battery condition assessment forms part of Honda's work on the used EV market, particularly as electric two-wheelers begin changing ownership. "Much like smartphones now provide battery-health indicators, EVs will also require reliable methods of evaluating battery condition," he said.He added that Honda was studying how similar customer behaviour could develop in the electric vehicle market. Battery condition and the acceptance of used vehicles are among the areas being examined.Honda's assessment comes as the company augments its electric two-wheeler portfolio in India. HMSI has introduced the Activa e:, QC1 and QC3, with the latter launched in September 2026 at Rs 1.35 lakh (ex-showroom). The QC3's initial rollout covers five cities, while Otani confirmed that additional electric products are under consideration without specifying their names, numbers or launch timelines. He added that Honda had more products under consideration, although customer requirements would determine which solutions were suitable.On the company's battery-swapping business, Otani said Honda was examining customer requirements across both business-to-business and private ownership segments. He said, "For B2B customers, battery swapping can be useful because there is no need to wait for charging, which can reduce working-time loss for professional users."For private customers, Otani pointed to battery                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             w w                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           ôÅ^!5Ç/13É ´K95148581762026-10-22T11:10:53ZTiger Shroff Firing Case: CCTV Footage Emerged Showing Accused Leaving Hotel With Blue Bag Allegedly Carrying Pistol Before Attack On Actor's CarFree Press Journalfreepressjournal.inhttps://www.freepressjournal.in/mumbai/tiger-shroff-firing-case-cctv-footage-emerged-showing-accused-leaving-hotel-with-blue-bag-allegedly-carrying-pistol-before-attack-on-actors-car-mumbai-newsengIndiaøÂıııııˆCCTV footage reportedly shows an accused leaving a hotel with a blue bag allegedly containing a pistol before the Tiger Shroff firing incident. Police suspect the attack was carried out at the behest of Lawrence Bishnoi gang member Anil Pandit. Several suspects have been arrested across states, while three accused remain in police custody until October 31 as investigations continue.Mumbai: Closed-circuit television footage from the investigation into the firing incident involving Bollywood actor Tiger Shroff reportedly shows one of the accused men leaving a hotel where he was allegedly staying before the attack.The footage shows the accused leaving the hotel on October 18 at around 4.12 pm, carrying a blue bag allegedly containing the pistol used in the attack on the actor's car, News18 reported.Accused Seen Leaving HotelThe report states that two other accused were subsequently seen leaving the premises on a motorcycle at around 4.13 pm on October 18.The footage has emerged as investigators examine the movements of the accused and the alleged transportation of weapons used in the firing incident.The police are analysing the sequence of events to establish how the suspects travelled to the location of the attack.Police Suspect Bishnoi Gang ConnectionThe Mumbai Police have alleged that the attack was carried out at the behest of Anil Pandit, identified by police as a member of the Lawrence Bishnoi gang. The case is being investigated by the Anti-Extortion Cell of the Mumbai Crime Branch.Fresh Arrests In HaryanaThe Mumbai Police arrested two more individuals from Haryana on Wednesday in connection with the firing incident.Earlier, on October 20, four suspects were arrested in Delhi and Haryana. The four accused have been identified as Kunal (21), Sumit (19), Radha (23) and Babita (23).According to Delhi Police, Kunal and Sumit were arrested in Sonipat, Haryana, while Radha and Babita were detained at New Delhi Railway Station.Women Allegedly Delivered WeaponsThe arrests followed an investigation into the alleged supply of weapons to the individuals involved in the firing.According to preliminary findings, Radha and Babita allegedly travelled to Mumbai to deliver a pistol and ammunition to those suspected of carrying out the attack. The two women reportedly returned to Delhi by train after the alleged delivery.Investigators are examining the suspected weapon supply network and the involvement of other individuals allegedly connected to the incident.Three Accused In Police CustodyMeanwhile, three accused identified as Phool Singh, Hrithik Kumar and Rehan Ansari were brought to Mumbai from Rajasthan's Kota in the early hours of Wednesday. A city court has remanded them to police custody till October 31.°<Å]!5Å!'-Å} æM95148601842026-10-22T11:12:29ZHonda studying used electric two-wheeler market before pivoting strategiesAutocar Indiaautocarindia.comhttps://www.autocarindia.com/bike-news/honda-studying-used-electric-two-wheeler-market-before-pivoting-strategies-441109engIndia?÷VVVVVWThe company is studying resale values, battery health and ownership patterns among the used electric two-wheeler market.Honda Motorcycle & Scooter India (HMSI) expects to gain a clearer understanding of    ø   ì ì                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         òiÅ_!5ÅQ%%Ç ¨q95148558222026-10-22T11:08:59ZUltraviolette unveils X-47 Venom electric bike on new 100V platform; gets 343 km range - CNBC TV18cnbctv18.comcnbctv18.comhttps://www.cnbctv18.com/auto/ultraviolette-unveils-x-47-venom-electric-bike-on-new-100v-platform-gets-343-km-range-20017150.htmengIndia?≥Ultraviolette launched the X-47 Venom electric motorcycle in India at an introductory price of ‚Çπ2.65 lakh. Built on a new 100V VX-100 platform, it features an 11 kWh battery delivering a 343 km range, a top speed of 155 km/h, and 12 kW fast charging. Bookings are open, with deliveries starting in No...Homegrown EV-maker Ultraviolette, on Thursday, launched the X-47 Venom electric motorcycle in India at an introductory price of ‚Çπ2.65 lakh for the first 5,000 vehicles, against a stated price of ‚Çπ3 lakh. Bookings are now open, with deliveries scheduled to begin in November 2026.The motorcycle is the first model to use the company's new VX-100 100V architecture and will also be launched in 20 countries across Europe.The X-47 Venom gets a new 11 kWh battery pack with an IDC-claimed range of 343 km. Its electric motor produces 30 kW (around 40 hp) and 550 Nm of torque at the wheel. Ultraviolette claims a top speed of 155 km/h and acceleration from 0 to 100 km/h in 5.9 seconds.The motorcycle weighs 180 kg, making it up to 20 kg lighter than the previous generation. The new battery pack has 15% lower weight and 25% higher energy density, according to the company.Lighter componentsThe new X-47 Venom introduces several hardware changes alongside the VX-100 architecture. These include the 11 kWh SRB-11 battery pack, the Light Fury motor and a new motor control unit (MCU).The Light Fury motor is 25% lighter than the previous unit, while the new MCU weighs 40% less. Together with the lighter battery, these changes contribute to the motorcycle's overall weight reduction and are designed to improve efficiency and performance.12 kW fast chargingThe VX-100 is a new 100V high-voltage platform designed to improve energy efficiency, thermal management, charging speed and range. Ultraviolette says the X-47 Venom supports 12 kW DC fast charging, a capability the company describes as unique in the electric two-wheeler industry.The battery system features an optimised thermal design intended to dissipate heat up to 50% faster, along with a cast-aluminium housing and IP67-rated protection against dust and water immersion. It also incorporates a pyrotechnic fuse, or PYRO FUSE, as part of its battery safety system.New featuresThe X-47 Venom gets cruise control, a seven-inch TFT LCD touchscreen, 200 mm of ground clearance and UV Hypersense Radar Intelligence. Other features include Park Assist, navigation integrated with Mappls, app connectivity, software updates and Violette AI.Ultraviolette will also introduce B-Max, a new rider safety vest, as part of its wider focus on rider safety.The company, founded in 2016 by Narayan Subramaniam and Niraj Rajmohan, develops electric vehicle platforms and battery technology. It has secured European certification for its motorcycles and exports to markets across Europe.   l l                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  ãÅ`!5Å!OÇ ëU95148556122026-10-22T11:08:50ZSimple Energy offers ‚Çπ10,000 discount on Wave electric scootersETAuto.comauto.economictimes.indiatimes.comhttps://auto.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/two-wheelers/simple-energy-offers-10000-discount-on-wave-electric-scooters/135116977engIndia?œüüüüü°The introductory offer on the Wave 3.7 kW and 5 kW TFT variants is valid until December 31 across North and East India, Madhya Pradesh and Gujarat.Simple Energy on Thursday announced a ‚Çπ10,000 introductory discount on its Wave 3.7 kW and 5 kW TFT electric scooter variants, bringing their effective prices down to ‚Çπ1.65 lakh and ‚Çπ1.80 lakh, respectively.The offer is valid until December 31, 2026, across North and East India, Madhya Pradesh and Gujarat. Customers can avail the discount through the company's touchpoints in these markets.Revised prices after discountThe Simple Wave 3.7 kW TFT variant is priced at ‚Çπ1,74,999, with the discount bringing its effective ex-showroom price to ‚Çπ1,64,999.The 5 kW TFT variant carries an all-India price of ‚Çπ1,89,999, which falls to ‚Çπ1,79,999 after the introductory benefit.Simple Energy unveiled the Wave range in September 2026. The electric scooter lineup offers multiple battery capacities, display options and connected features.The company said the Wave features a 920 mm seat and 70 litres of storage capacity, targeting everyday commuting needs.    ‚  ‚                                                                                                                                                                                                                        ûÅa!5ÅU[!Å% ∏95148547322026-10-22T11:08:31ZUltraviolette X-47 Venom Launched At ‚Çπ2.65 Lakh: Bigger Battery, New Platform, Faster AccelerationCarToq - India‚Äôs #1 auto content sitecartoq.comhttps://www.cartoq.com/bike-news/ultraviolette-x47-venom-launch-343km-range/engIndiaøú!Ultraviolette has launched the X-47 Venom at an introductory price of ‚Çπ2.65 lakh (ex-showroom), limited to the first 5,000 buyers. It arrives with an 11 kWh battery, a claimed 343 Km range, 12kW DC fast-charging capability and a new electrical platform. Bookings are open from 4 PM on October 22nd, with deliveries expected from November.The Venom uses Ultraviolette's new VX-100 architecture, operating at 100 volts. It is rated at 30kW (around 40bhp), can reportedly reach 155 kmph and has a claimed 0-100kmph time of 5.9 seconds. The manufacturer quotes a weight of 180kg, up to 20kg below its earlier motorcycle.A Bigger Battery, But Only 20km More RangeThe new SRB-11 battery has 11kWh of usable capacity. That is larger than the 10.3kWh unit in the existing long-range X-47 Recon, which is rated for 323km on the IDC test cycle. The Venom's 343km claim represents an additional 20km, or just over six percent. Actual highway range could be considerably lower than either laboratory figure.What makes the battery development interesting is the packaging. Ultraviolette claims the pack is 15 percent lighter and 25 percent more energy-dense than the earlier design. It also uses a revised cooling arrangement, which the company says doubles cooling performance. Those changes could be useful when riding quickly for extended periods or charging repeatedly, although independent temperature and range testing is still needed.The Venom supports DC charging at up to 12kW. That gives it a potentially useful touring advantage over electric motorcycles dependent on slower charging, provided compatible chargers are available along the route. Ultraviolette has not supplied a verified 10-80 percent charging time, so buyers cannot yet calculate the length of a typical highway stop.Faster Than The Old X-47The existing long-range X-47 also makes 30 kW, so the Venom does not increase peak output. Its claimed 0-100 kmph sprint of 5.9 seconds, however, is substantially quicker than the earlier X-47 Recon's stated 8.1 seconds.The claimed top speed rises from 145kmph to 155kmph. Lower weight, revised gearing and power delivery may explain the improvement, but their individual contributions have not been disclosed.The updated Light Fury motor spins at up to 9,000 rpm. Ultraviolette claims it uses 50 percent less rare-earth material and weighs 25-30 percent less. Its controller is also smaller and lighter. The new battery adds a pyro-fuse intended to isolate the high-voltage circuit rapidly in the event of a serious electrical fault, while the enclosure carries an IP67 rating.Ballistic riding mode remains available until the battery drops to 10 percent, and regenerative braking can now operate from the beginning of a full charge. Both changes should make the bike's responses more consistent across a journey.The ‚Çπ2.65 Lakh QuestionThe Venom retains the X-47 family's 77GHz rear radar, which supports blind-spot, lane-change, overtaking and approaching-vehicle alerts. These are warnings for the rider, not a system that automatically brakes the motorcycle. Other equipment includes a 7-inch touchscreen, Mappls navigation, cruise control, rear-camera parking assistance and over-the-air updates.For context, the existing entry X-47 is listed from ‚Çπ2.49 lakh with a 7.1kWh battery and 211km claimed range. The Venom costs only ‚Çπ16,000 more at its introductory price, despite its much larger battery. This comparison is attractive, although the two models have different equipment and the post-introductory Venom price is not yet known.   n  _n                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  °JÅc!5Å+#'Ç æM95148536682026-10-22T11:08:05ZSamir Kochhar recalls Tiger Shroff at daughter's birthday minutes before attackIndia Todayindiatoday.inhttps://www.indiatoday.in/movies/celebrities/story/tiger-shroff-shooting-samir-kochhar-daughter-birthday-party-juhu-3022711-2026-10-22engIndia?™!Samir Kochhar recounted meeting Tiger Shroff shortly before gunfire incident outside a Juhu football ground. The incident has sparked concerns over safety measures in the city.Actor Samir K   ≈ëÅb!5Å!!)Åi û-95148543502026-10-22T11:08:26ZBenelli TRK 552, TRK 552X Launched in India; Prices Start at Rs. 7.22 Lakhcarandbikecarandbike.comhttps://www.carandbike.com/news/benelli-trk-552-trk-552x-launched-in-india-prices-start-at-rs-722-lakh-3222433engIndia?™!* TRK 552 powered by 554 cc, paralllel-twin engine* Liquid-cooled engine makes 52 bhp, 49 Nm* TRK 552 X gets tubeless wire-spoke wheelsBenelli India has launched the new Benelli TRK 552, which will replace the TRK 502 as the brand's middleweight adventure touring motorcycle. The new TRK 552 is powered by a bigger 554 cc, liquid-cooled, parallel-twin engine and is offered in two variants - a standard TRK 552 with cast alloy wheels and a TRK 552 X with tubeless wire spoke wheels. The TRK 552 has been priced at Rs. 7,22,000 (Ex-showroom), while the TRK 552X has been priced at Rs. 7,74,000 (Ex-showroom).A top-of-the-line TRK 552 X Limited Edition in a unique yellow colourway has also been introduced. The TRK 552 X Limited Edition only gets the special colour option and is priced at Rs.7,90,000 (Ex-showroom).Benelli TRK 552 EngineThe new TRK 552 appears to be an evolved motorcycle compared to the TRK 502 it replaces. It's powered by a 554 cc, liquid-cooled, parallel-twin engine which delivers 52 bhp at 8,500 rpm and 49 Nm of peak torque at 6,500 rpm. The engine is paired to a six-speed transmission with a slipper clutch.Benelli TRK 552 ChassisBoth the TRK 552 and TRK 552 X are built around a tubular steel trellis frame and aluminium swingarm, and suspension duties are handled by a 50 mm Marzocchi USD fork and a preload adjustable rear monoshock. Braking is handled by twin 320 mm floating discs and a 260 mm rear disc, supported by Bosch dual-channel ABS.Benelli TRK 552 FeaturesOn the features side, a 5-inch TFT instrument cluster takes centre stage, equipped with Bluetooth connectivity and turn-by-turn navigation, as well as a tyre pressure navigation system (TPMS). According to Benelli India, the chassis has been upgraded to accommodate the heavier weight of the TRK 552. At 235 kg, the TRK 552 is expected to be a heavy adventure tourer, considering its engine size and segment positioning.    ochhar was celebrating his daughter Sara's ninth birthday at a football ground in Mumbai's Juhu when gunshots rang out, turning what was meant to be a fun-filled Sunday into a frightening ordeal. Tiger Shroff, who had been playing football on the adjoining half of the ground, was targeted in the shooting. While the actor was unharmed, his driver sustained injuries during the attack.Recounting the incident to India Today, Samir said the birthday party was nearing its end when Tiger left the venue. The actor, who regularly plays football with Tiger and the All-Stars group, said the gunshots initially sounded like firecrackers, given that it was the festive season. It was only when people at the gate began screaming that they realised something serious had happened."Tiger and the gang all walked in earlier. They met all the kids, met my daughter, before heading for the game. And as the party was almost finishing, Tiger walked back, met a few more kids, they took a few more pictures of them. And then I said, 'You want to eat something?' And of course, being on a strict diet as always, he said no, and sat in his car, and headed out in front of me," he recalled.Samir said Tiger's car had travelled only a short distance when they heard loud sounds. With firecrackers being common during the festive season, the group initially did not suspect that gunshots had been fired."Then, just about 30 seconds later, the people at the gate who witnessed the whole scenario started screaming, saying that Tiger has been shot. And there have been bullets that have been fired. We went running to the gate to see what had happened. Tiger had just sped off at that point, but we were shocked to realise what had happened," he added.The sudden commotion left the children at the birthday party frightened and confused. With some parents yet to arrive, Samir said the adults had to take charge of the situation and ensure that the children were safe. Bunty Sajdeh left for the police station before joining Tiger, Samir said.Calling the shooting bizarre and unexpected, Samir said the Sunday football meet had been a regular fixture for the group for the past five to six years. He said no one had any idea that something like this could happen and expressed shock that there had apparently been no prior intelligence about the incident.The shooting has also raised questions about whether the group would feel safe returning to the ground for its regular Sunday matches. Samir said it was too early to discuss what would happen next, as everyone was still processing the incident and feeling relieved that Tiger was safe."So, as of now, people are really thankful and grateful that nothing has happened to him. And I am very happy that, personally, from my end, the kids are all okay," he said.Samir recalled that some people present at the ground had seen the shooters, but the incident unfolded so quickly that its gravity was not immediately apparent to everyone. The group understood what had happened only after people began screaming at the gate and they saw the damage.Asked whether he had managed to speak to Tiger after the incident, Samir said he had been keeping in touch through Bunty Walia and had heard that the actor was doing okay.The shooting has also raised fresh concerns about the safety of members of the film industry, particularly in Mumbai, where celebrities have faced threats and attacks in recent years. For Samir, the fact that the incident took place in a busy neighbourhood like Juhu, in broad daylight, was particularly disturbing.Asked how he viewed the incident as a celebrity and a citizen of Mumbai, and what needed to change, Samir said, "See, I think the authorities best know what to do. But obviously, something is amiss somewhere. Because it's no                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               ˝  ˝                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   ùÅd!5Åg!ÇY ∂K95148535032026-10-22T11:08:00ZThis Indian Newspaper Boy's Insane Throwing Skills Have People Asking If He's A Professional Athlete | PhotosNews18news18.comhttps://www.news18.com/photogallery/viral/this-indian-newspaper-boys-insane-throwing-skills-have-people-asking-if-hes-a-professional-athlete-photos-ws-l-10393081.htmlengIndia?Õùùùùù†Every morning, a newspaper delivery boy makes his way through quiet streets, but his delivery style is anything but ordinary. With a quick flick of his wrist, he sends newspapers flying straight to their destinations. His throws are so accurate that viewers are watching his videos again and again. What started as a regular morning job has now turned him into an internet favourite. (Image: Instagram/paper_boy_deep)Meet Paper Boy Deep, The Newspaper Throwing Pro: Known as Paper Boy Deep on Instagram, he has gained attention for his unusual way of delivering newspapers. He rides through neighbourhoods, folds papers with one hand and throws them with impressive accuracy. Some videos show newspapers landing through narrow openings, while others capture papers reaching residents waiting at their doors. His clips have attracted millions of views, with viewers amazed by how easily he pulls off these throws. (Image: Instagram/paper_boy_deep)His Throws Look Like They Need No Effort: There are no special effects or fancy tricks behind Deep's deliveries. He relies on practice, timing and a steady hand to get the job done. He can send a rolled newspaper spinning down a hallway or land one in a resident's outstretched hand. These moments have left viewers wondering how he manages such accuracy while moving quickly through his daily route. For Deep, though, it appears to be part of his regular work. (Image: Instagram/paper_boy_deep)The Internet Has Turned His Skills Into Memes: Deep's videos have sparked plenty of jokes and praise online. Some viewers have joked that newspaper throwing should become an Olympic sport, while others have compared his throws to the moves of famous athletes. One person called his newspapers "GPS-enabled newspapers," while another said he had "cracked geometry before breakfast." Marvel superhero comparisons have also appeared, along with comments praising his confidence before 7 am. (Image: Instagram/paper_boy_deep)He Gets The Job Done On Two Wheels: Deep delivers newspapers while riding both a scooter and a bicycle. He moves through streets filled with cars and bikes, throwing papers towards their intended spots without slowing down much. His videos show how much control and practice the job can require. The way he folds each newspaper, aims his throw and flicks his wrist has become a talking point, with viewers studying his movements like sports fans analysing a star player. (Image: Instagram/paper_boy_deep)Viewers See More Than Just A Viral Video:Beyond the jokes and memes, many people admire the effort behind Deep's work. His videos offer a break from social media stunts that rely on expensive setups or staged moments. He is simply showing a skill he has built through daily practice. Some viewers have even joked about Netflix making a documentary called "The Last Paper Throw." Their reactions show how an ordinary job can impress people when someone does it exceptionally well. (Image: Instagram/paper_boy_deep)Paper Boy Deep Shows The Power Of Everyday Skills: Deep has not needed dramatic speeches, luxury cars or a carefully planned online image to get noticed. He continues to deliver newspapers with the same focus and confidence that made his clips popular. His story has reminded viewers that talent can show up in everyday work, not just on a sports field or a stage. For Deep, a normal morning shift has become a chance to show what practice and skill can achieve. (Image: Instagram/paper_boy_deep)    r 18.His driver sustained injuries.Ten arrests have been made so far.Sunday evening brought a harrowing scene to Mumbai's Juhu area as Bollywood actor Tiger Shroff walked out of a football match at the Pushpa Narsee Ground. Assailants stepped forward and unleashed a barrage of seven gunshots directly at him, while a third partner stood anvil on a waiting motorcycle. As panic transpired, the trio scurried onto the bike and sped off, eventually ditching it near the Andheri West railway station to catch a train bound for Jaipur. In the chaos of the shooting, Tiger managed to escape with minor scrapes, though his driver took a bullet to the hand and a bystander was hurt in the chest. Keeping his composure amidst the adrenaline, Tiger immediately slid into the driver's seat and rushed his injured driver straight to Nanavati Hospital.Police Developments, Trail Of ArrestsPolice quickly initiated a massive, multi-state manhunt. The results indicated the attack was carefully planned. Investigators found that the shooters had been tailing Tiger for over ten days, keeping tabs on his daily routine, gym workouts, and football games. CCTV footage and call logs procured by The Indian Express establish the trio was lying in wait outside the Juhu football pitch as early as October 11. An investigating officer confirmed they had arrived fully prepared to open fire that evening, but aborted the attempt when the actor failed to turn up. Shroff never got threat calls before the shooting.The first major break came on October 19 in Kota, Rajasthan, where police tracked down and arrested the two main gunmen along with a getaway driver. The investigation soon unraveled a much larger network, leading to eight total arrests across Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, and Delhi. Among those caught were two women accused of smuggling the guns by train all the way from Amritsar to hand them off to the shooters in Mumbai. Three men, identified as Hritik alias Roshan (22), from Begusarai in Bihar, Rehan (18), from Sonipat in Haryana, and Phool Singh (18), also from Haryana, were arrested within 24 hours on Monday. Arrest Tally RisesThe arrest tally in the Tiger Shroff shooting case climbed to 10 on Wednesday as Mumbai Police collared two key weapon brokers, Samir and Jatin, shortly after Gujarat authorities caught hold of a Lawrence Bishnoi operative. This latest round of busts ensues Tuesday's multi-state sweeps in Haryana and Delhi that netted four co-conspirators. Investigators revealed that Jatin had previously journeyed to Amritsar alongside accomplices Kunal and Sumit specifically to source the firearms used in the assault.Explained: Why Tiger Shroff Was Targeted By Lawrence Bishnoi Gang: The Feud With Salman Khan That Hovers Over BollywoodLawrence Bishnoi Gang Assumes ResponsibilitySoon after the failed assassination, jailed gangster Lawrence Bishnoi claimed responsibility through his associate Anil Pandit. In an audio clip sent to various security agencies, there was a clear, unequivocal threat, "In the entire film industry, whoever works without aligning with us, or works with Salman Khan, will be killed. This is the first and last warning." Salman Khan's name has been tied to threats from the Lawrence Bishnoi gang for a decade. The aggression goes back to 1998 blackbuck poaching case in Rajasthan, where the animal is considered sacred by the Bishnoi community, to which the dreaded gangster belongs. Khan was in Rajasthan to shoot for the one of his blockbusters Hum Saath Saath Hain. During the filming, two blackbucks were allegedly killed near Kankani village in Jodhpur. The superstar was among the actors accused in the case.2022 and 2023 saw threats being levied at Khan. Security has been beefed up at the superstar's residence after the Shroff attack.Tiger Shroff's Team Hits Back At                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 ]  ]                                                                               ó=Åg!5Å!OÇ ™95148325672026-10-22T10:53:39ZUltraviolette launches X-47 Venom electric motorcycle at ‚Çπ2.65 lakhETAuto.comauto.economictimes.indiatimes.comhttps://auto.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/two-wheelers/ultraviolette-launches-x-47-venom-electric-motorcycle-at-2-65-lakh/135116036engIndia?π The new electric motorcycle introduces the company's 100V VX-100 platform, offers a claimed 343 km range and supports 12 kW DC fast charging.Ultraviolette Automotive on Thursday launched the X-47 Venom electric motorcycle at an introductory price of ‚Çπ2.65 lakh. The model is the first motorcycle built on the company's new VX-100 100V architecture.Bookings opened on October 22 at 4 pm, while deliveries are scheduled to begin in November 2026. The introductory price applies to the first 5,000 customers, the company said.The X-47 Venom is powered by a 30 kW electric motor and offers an IDC-certified range of 343 km. It has a claimed top speed of 155 kmph and accelerates from 0 to 100 kmph in 5.9 seconds. The motorcycle weighs 180 kg and produces 550 Nm of torque at the wheel.New platform brings battery and charging upgradesThe VX-100 platform combines the newly developed UV LIGHTFURY motor, SRB-11 battery technology and a next-generation motor control unit.Ultraviolette said the SRB-11 battery offers up to 20 per cent higher energy density and twice the cooling performance. Its thermal management system is designed to dissipate heat up to 50 per cent faster.The motorcycle supports 12 kW external DC fast charging, alongside a 1.6 kW onboard charger. The company describes it as India's only electric two-wheeler capable of 12 kW DC charging.Battery safety features include a pyro-fuse designed to isolate the circuit within microseconds when a critical fault is detected. The battery pack also incorporates a cell-level fuse and a battery management system.The motor control unit is claimed to be twice as compact and offer three times the power density of its predecessor.Radar technology and new rider safety gearThe X-47 Venom features a 7-inch TFT touchscreen, cruise control, integrated front and rear dashcams, and UV Hypersense radar technology.The 77 GHz rear radar can monitor traffic up to 200 metres away. Its functions include blind-spot monitoring, lane-change assistance, overtake alerts and rear-collision warnings. The motorcycle also features rear-camera-based park assist, Mappls navigation and connected functionsAlongside the motorcycle, Ultraviolette introduced B-MAX, a reusable rider safety vest. The company claims the vest provides 86 per cent force attenuation and protection across the collar, back, chest, sides and hips. It is designed for reuse up to five times.Ultraviolette CEO and co-founder Narayan Subramaniam said the VX-100 platform would provide a technology foundation for future products across segments and markets.The company will launch the X-47 Venom in Europe on November 3 at EICMA 2026 in Milan, Italy.†pÅf!5Å;[!ÅA Ω/95148356202026-10-22T10:56:33ZBajaj Pulsar N250SS Launched At ‚Çπ1.59 Lakh: Is It Worth ‚Çπ14,000 More Than The N250?CarToq - India‚Äôs #1 auto content sitecartoq.comhttps://www.cartoq.com/bike-news/bajaj-pulsar-n250ss-launch-six-speed-gearbox-tft-display/engIndia?¡ëëëëëëBajaj has launched the Pulsar N250SS at ‚Çπ1,59,406 (ex-showroom, Delhi), ‚Çπ14,136 above the regular N250 priced at ‚Çπ1,45,270. That premium brings a revised four-valve engine, a six-speed gearbox, a    À°iÅe!5ÅOG-Ç	 æM95148477092026-10-22T11:04:51ZTiger Shroff Shooting Case: 10 Arrests, Gang Links And the Pivot From Salman Khan | Outlook Indiahttps://www.outlookindia.com/outlookindia.comhttps://www.outlookindia.com/art-entertainment/tiger-shroff-shooting-case-10-arrests-gang-links-and-the-pivot-from-salman-khanengIndiaø’UUUUUWSo far, ten arrests have been made in the assassination attempt on Tiger Shroff on October 18Tiger Shroff narrowly survived an assassination attempt on Octobe   «‡ Æ‚!¸®∏»ÿË¯(8HXhxòà®∏»ÿË¯0@P`pÄê†∞¿–‡	Ïr  û¸⁄∏ñöÍ˙*é\™x6‘Ç‚Ú"2BRbrî§¥ƒÊˆ&HXhäöºÃ‹Ï¸,<Ln~†∞¿–:JZjzä¨ºÃ‹Ï&6FVfvÜ® Ï   . > N ^ n ~ é ∞ ¿ – ‡"2BRbÉí∞°øŒ›˝		,	;	J	Y	h	w	Ü	ï	§	¬	≥	—	‡	Ô	˛+:IXgv≥¬—Ò -<KZzâòß≈∂‘„ÚÇ‘6x™\é‡Ú(öñ∏⁄¸ û rÏ	hà>ã#oÄë¢‡i                !9514847709 Â!9514853503 ‰!9514853668 „!9514854350 ‚!9514854732 ·!9514855612 ‡!9514855822 ﬂ!9514858176 ﬁ!9514860184 ›!9514862357 ‹!9514835620 Ê €!9514866682 ⁄!9514867003 Ÿ!9514868763 ÿ!9514870431 ◊!9514832567 Á ÷!9514873814 ’!9514879899 ‘!9514882062 ”!95148821