                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   ä'Åm!5Å+!)Åw ê)95148125732026-10-22T10:38:08ZYouth injured in bike crash caught with MDMA during medical check in MalappuramOnManoramaonmanorama.comhttps://www.onmanorama.com/news/kerala/2026/10/22/youth-arrested-with-mdma-after-bike-accident-in-perinthalmanna.htmlengIndiaø’UUUUUWWhat began as a routine emergency room examination following a road accident took a dramatic turn when doctors at a medical college in Perinthalmanna discovered synthetic drugs hidden in an injured patient's pocket.The episode began unfolding after 21-year-old Sibin, a resident of Amayur near Koppam, met with a motorcycle crash at Edappal late at night. He was rushed with injuries to MES Medical College Hospital in Perinthalmanna for emergency treatment. However, while examining the patient, the doctor noticed a velvet pouch tucked inside his trouser pocket containing white crystalline granules. Suspecting the substance to be illegal narcotics, the hospital management immediately alerted the local police.Remanded in hospitalA police team led by Inspector Sumesh Sudhakaran arrived at the hospital and inspected the pouch, seizing 21.42 grams of MDMA. Following the seizure, a magistrate visited the hospital facility to formally place the accused under judicial remand. Officers said a detailed investigation was underway.   ß ß                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             ñUÅn!5Å!#'ÇG ®?95148124962026-10-22T10:38:04ZTiger Shroff's team shares video of actor's burn marks after firing attackIndia Todayindiatoday.inhttps://www.indiatoday.in/movies/celebrities/story/tiger-shroff-team-shares-video-of-actor-gunpowder-burn-injury-marks-after-firing-attack-3022687-2026-10-22engIndiaøªActor Tiger Shroff's team shared a video showing burn marks on his arm following the firing incident. The post comes after trolls questioned the attack's authenticity, pointing to pictures of the actor's seemingly uninjured skin.Actor Tiger Shroff's team has shared a glimpse of the gunpowder burn marks on his hand following the firing incident outside a football stadium on October 18. The video, posted on Instagram with the caption "God is Kind", shows small, red, dot-like marks across the actor's arm.The post appears to respond to trolls questioning the authenticity of the attack after pictures of Tiger with seemingly unmarked skin surfaced online just two days after photos of him with heavily bandaged injuries went viral.Earlier, Tiger's team issued a statement after the incident and clarified reports circulating online about misinformation, scripted narratives, and AI-generated content spreading across digital platforms.In the statement, Team Tiger expressed concern over unauthorised content, saying, "We would like to categorically clarify that Tiger Shroff has made no statements, released no videos, and authorised no communication regarding the recent incident or any related developments. It has come to our attention that several social media pages and digital platforms are circulating fabricated stories, AI-generated videos and scripted narratives, falsely attributed to Tiger. These are completely false, unauthorised and intended to mislead the public (sic)."The team warned that those creating or circulating misleading content could face legal consequences. It said it was taking cognisance of such instances and reserved the right to pursue legal remedies, urging the public to rely only on verified updates from official channels.The incident took place on October 18 when unidentified men riding a motorcycle opened fire at Shroff's car. The actor escaped unhurt, while his driver and a fan sustained injuries. Three shooters, two from Haryana and one from Bihar, were arrested by Kota Police in Rajasthan in connection with the incident. They were subsequently handed over to Mumbai Police for further investigation.Maharashtra Deputy Chief Minister Eknath Shinde subsequently met the actor at his residence and assured a thorough review of security protocols for high-profile film personalities.Meanwhile, gangster Anil Pandit, who allegedly operates from the United States, has emerged as a key suspect in the case. Police are probing his alleged links to more than a dozen extortion cases and targeted attacks in Delhi and Haryana.   ˛ ˛                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    ì~Åo!5ÅÅa §95148111852026-10-22T10:37:06ZNDTV World Summit 2026: What Nikhil Kamath Said On Inheritance TaxNDTVndtv.comhttps://www.ndtv.com/world-news/ndtv-world-summit-2026-what-nikhil-kamath-said-on-inheritance-tax-12219521engIndia?“RRRRRUNDTV World Summit 2026: Nikhil Kamath also spoke about the Gini coefficient - which measures income inequality or wealth inequality in a society.Nikhil Kamath at the NDTV World Summit 2026New Delhi:Zerodha co-founder Nikhil Kamath on Thursday backed the inheritance tax, saying it is "not the worst idea" in the world.Speaking at NDTV World Summit 2026, he said, "I don't like the idea of taxing somebody who is earning money in his lifetime and incentivising that but taxing people between generations when wealth is being passed through; I don't know in what manner. It has to be done in a manner where it's not an unrealized gain. It doesn't mean selling equity in your company and having to pay those as tax. They might be an efficient way to do it. It's probably not the worst idea in the world."He recalled how he was earlier "trolled" when he had shared his views on inheritance tax.An inheritance tax is a tax paid by the beneficiary who receives money, property, or assets from a person who has died.Kamath also spoke about the Gini coefficient - which measures income inequality or wealth inequality in a society."It's doing badly in most pockets of the world, but in some countries it seems to be changing for the positive. It was quite rare, so I was kind of researching that...I feel like as long as wage growth in society is not going to keep up with asset price inflation, logically it's impossible for wealth inequality to come down. The government has to probably be interventionist in some way to make this Gini coefficient better," the investor and entrepreneur told NDTV's Editor-in-Chief and CEO Rahul Kanwal."...To the rich people in this room, it's not even altruism, but it could be self-serving to solve this problem when you can, before you reach a point where you have to. I think it's a lot worse trying to change it then," Kamath added.Kamath is best known as the co-founder of Zerodha, one of India's largest stockbroking firms. He has also invested in several businesses, including Licious, Nourish You, Pee Safe, Subko, and Ather, an electric scooter maker.How may i help you todayShow full article