ime through 2028, we are looking at EBITDA breakeven," said Narayan Subramaniam, Co-Founder and CEO of Ultraviolette.The Bengaluru-based startup, backed by TVS Motor, Qualcomm Ventures and Zoho Corporation, had been earlier targeting to reach the break-even in 2026.The company, which currently has presence in pan-India and in 20 European countries, is planning to enter the US market in 2027, while also evaluating opportunities in Latin America and Southeast Asia as part of its international expansion strategy."We are looking at Latin America and the US as well," Subramaniam said.Ultraviolette's international business currently contributes around 15 per cent of its overall volumes, with Europe accounting for the bulk of its overseas presence. However, as part of its expansion, it expects the share to rise to approximately 25 per cent over the years.With the recent $85 million funding round in September, led by Yali Capital and TDK Ventures, the company plans to expand its production, develop new electric vehicle platforms, and pursue international market expansion.Ultraviolette plans to cater to diverse buyers through its portfolio across four segments, including sports motorcycles, street and dirt motorcycles, crossover motorcycles and electric scootersThe company on Thursday launched its X-47 Venom crossover motorcycle, and expects to roll out Shockwave motorcycle and the Tesseract electric scooter in the next year. With this pipeline of launches, Ultraviolette targets to increase its monthly volumes to close to 10,000 units by around this time next year.Along with this, Ultraviolette plans to expand its manufacturing footprint through a new facility in Hosur, Tamil Nadu, which will initially manufacture scooters. Its existing Karnataka facility has an annual production capacity of up to 50,000 vehicles and will continue to support motorcycle production."Our scooters themselves will quickly ramp up to, say, 10,000 units a month. So only for that alone, we will need a production capacity of more than 100,000," Subramaniam added.Alongside manufacturing expansion, Ultraviolette is targeting a retail network of 100 stores across India by the first quarter of 2027, compared with around 58 currently.    and core sector activity, according to the Moneycontrol Eco Pulse. The Moneycontrol Eco Pulse Index rose to 56.3 in September from 55.7 in August, remaining firmly in expansion territory and reversing some of the moderation seen in the previous month. A reading above 50 indicates expansion. The improvement was broad-based, with purchasing managers' indices strengthening alongside automobile sales, credit growth, port traffic and infrastructure output. However, softer fuel consumption, a sharp slowdown in hiring and higher demand for work under the rural employment guarantee programme pointed to pockets of weakness. Autos drive consumption recovery Automobile sales were among the strongest contributors to the September improvement. Four-wheeler sales grew 32.4 percent year-on-year, accelerating from 19.7 percent in August. Two-wheeler sales growth picked up to 33.5 percent from 20.5 percent, while three-wheeler registrations rose 63.5 percent compared with 48.8 percent in the previous month. Tractor sales also recovered, growing 14.4 percent in September after increasing just 2.5 percent in August, providing a stronger signal from rural consumption. The improvement across vehicle categories suggests discretionary spending strengthened during the month after losing some momentum in August. Fuel consumption, however, was less buoyant. Petrol consumption growth moderated slightly to 7.9 percent from 8.2 percent, while diesel demand growth eased to 5.6 percent from 6.8 percent. What's up and what's down Business activity improved across both manufacturing and services during September. The Manufacturing PMI rose to 55.1 from 52.8 in August, while the Services PMI increased to 55.2 from 54.1, pointing to a stronger pace of economic activity. The pickup in manufacturing was accompanied by stronger infrastructure activity as well. Core sector output growth accelerated to a 30-month high of 7.9 percent in September from 5.9 percent in August. Major port cargo traffic growth also accelerated to 12.7 percent from 8.4 percent, suggesting greater movement of goods through the economy. Non-food credit growth accelerated to 21.4 percent in September from 19.8 percent in August, while aggregate deposit growth strengthened to 19.4 percent from 17.6 percent. UPI transaction volumes continued to expand more than 22 percent year-on-year, though growth was broadly unchanged from August. Credit card payments were a notable exception, contracting 3.1 percent during the month after growing 8.5 percent in August, indicating that strength in household spending was not uniform across payment channels. The labour market presented a more mixed picture. Growth in the Naukri JobSpeak Index slowed sharply to 1.7 percent in September from 13.7 percent in August, suggesting a loss of momentum in white-collar hiring. Demand for work under the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA) also increased 36 percent from a year earlier, compared with a 9 percent increase in August. Higher demand for work under the scheme may indicate greater stress in rural labour markets. Urban labour indicators also softened marginally. The unemployment rate increased to 6.9 percent from 6.8 percent, while youth unemployment rose to 19 percent from 18.7 percent. Inflation bears watching Wholesale inflation accelerated further in September, rising to 11.4 per cent from 9.9 per cent in August. While stronger economic activity supports growth, the pickup in wholesale prices could become a constraint if higher input costs start feeding into manufacturing margins and consumer prices. Earlier this month, the Reserve Bank of India raised the policy rate for the first time in three years to 5.5 percent from 5.25 percent. Economists expect more rate hikes as prices climb in the coming months. Retail inflation crossed the 5 percent mark in the new series, as it                                                                                                                                                                                                              ¥ ¥                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          îHÅr!5Å'-Ç- §795148064922026-10-22T10:33:19ZMagadi Road murder: Husband arrested for killing wife over illicit affairDeccan Heralddeccanherald.comhttps://www.deccanherald.com/india/karnataka/bengaluru/bengaluru-husband-kills-wife-suspecting-infidelity-dumps-body-in-stormwater-drain-4191290engIndiaø——————“Bengaluru: The Magadi Road police have cracked the case of a woman, whose decomposed body was found in a stormwater drain on October 13. The case was solved nine days after the police learnt that she was allegedly murdered by her husband, suspecting her of having an illicit relationship.The deceased has been identified as Ruksana, 25, a native of Bihar . Her husband, Mohammed Irfaz, also a native of Bihar, allegedly killed her on October 10. He was taken into custody, following an investigation, police said.After questioning the suspect, investigators established that he had allegedly murdered his wife and dumped her body in the drain..Bengaluru: Fire guts Uttarahalli flat, short circuit suspected . A senior police officer said that Ruksana and Irfaz had married about a year ago. Ruksana had been married earlier and has a child. After separating from her first husband, she reportedly met Irfaz in Bihar, and the two fell in love.. The couple moved to Bengaluru about six months ago and worked in garment factories.Irfaz allegedly grew suspicious of Ruksana because she frequently spoke to people over the phone. The issue reportedly led to frequent arguments between them.On October 10, an argument between the couple escalated, during which Irfaz allegedly struck Ruksana on the head with a stone, killing her instantly.To transport the body, the accused allegedly broke the handle lock of a motorcycle, belonging to an unidentified person. He placed the body inside a bag, positioned it in the middle of the motorcycle and then pushed the vehicle before dumping the body in the stormwater drain.After the decomposed body was found on October 13 in Magadi Road police station limits, the police formed a special team to establish the identity of the woman and trace the accused.During the investigation, the police received information from local residents that a man from Gopalapura had reported his wife missing.When Irfaz was questioned, he allegedly told the police that Ruksana had returned to her native place in Bihar and was not using a mobile phone. Suspecting something amiss in his explanation, the police took him into custody and further interrogated him, during which he allegedly confessed to the murder, police said.The Magadi Road police is further investigating the case.   … ∫…                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             °eÅt!5ÅI%)Ç	 æq95148033622026-10-22T10:31:08ZBengaluru's Industrial & Logistics Supply Jumps 73.3%: Check the Top 5 Micro-Markets in DemandTrade Brainstradebrains.inhttps://tradebrains.in/money/bengalurus-industrial-logistics-supply-jumps-733-check-the-top-5-micro-markets-in-demand-12695680engIndia?ªBengaluru's industrial and logistics real estate market recorded a 73.3% year-on-year increase in new supply to 5.2 million sq. ft. in H1 2026, according to Savills India. Industrial   ◊îBÅs!5Å!%Åu §s95148060642026-10-22T10:32:56ZDucati Hypermotard V2 Price in India, S